      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Royalty at West Dean Park.— The christening of the son and heir of Mr. and Mrs. William James will take place about the middle of the month, and King Edward has promised to act as godfather to the fortunate infant. Next month, the King and Queen of Spain,and Princess Henry of Battenberg, will pay a visit to Mr. and Mrs. William James, at West Dean Park, and as at present arranged, private theatricals will be a feature of the visit. Mrs. W. James, who is a great favourite with King Edward and Queen Alexandra, is one of the cleverest actresses in Society. She always takes a prominent part in the Christmas theatricals at Chats- worth and is a talented step-dancer. Society Women's Pets.—lt is a rare event for Lady Aberdeen to take a walk without her numerous retinue of little skye terriers, and so devoted is she to her canine pets that she is having models of her principal favourites made by an Italian sculptor. Cats, dogs, and birds, are the creatures usually selected by Society women, upon whom to lavish affection, but baby lions. monkeys, lizards,tortoises,parrots, owls,snakes, and even rats, have figured on the list of" pets." The ladies of our Royal Family are particularly fond of cats and Princess Victoria of Schleswig-Holsteen keeps them well supplied from her wonderful "cattery" at Cumber- land Lodge, Windsor. The " cattery " is a real houee, with windows and a door boasting a knocker, and inside it is fitted up with the utmost luxury for the animals which are superb of their kind. The Craze for Diabolo.—lt almost looks as if diabolo is to become as popular as the ping-pong craze of five or six years ago. It "caught on "in Paris in the early part of this year, but with us it is only recently that it has risen above the dignity of a toy for children. But we are all more or less children over our games, and in this respect some people never do grow up. It is evident that we cannot go for any length of time with- out some silly craze or other, and there are people who have taken to flying kites, which suggests the benefits of fresh air without undue excitement. It is quite possible that kite-flying will become a popular fad in fashionable circles, although diabolo seems to have caught the fancy of the moment. The time of year, however, is not very opportune for out-door games, and it is possible that the enthusiasm for it will not survive the chilly nutumn and winter. If it does. it will surely be all the rage next spring. Our Difficult Climate.—Our climate, never an easy one from a "how to dress" point of view, has thia year surely surpassed itself. At Easter we needed muslins, during the summer winter clothing, and in September, when our autumn toilettes lay ready and waiting, we were donning the thinnest garments we had on hand. Provided we were careful to wear warm wraps in the morning and after sundown, the oppor- tunity was an excellent one for unearthing and exhibit- ing some of our unwonted summer gowns, but that vast numbers of girls and women were inexcusably careless in this way, the doctors can amply testify. White Felt Hats.—There is quite an epidemic of white felts in shopland, but if we can afford them, the cream and champagne tints are much better purchases. In the first place they are more becoming to the average complexion than pure white, and then again they appear better style when trimmed. The only thing against cream and champagne felts is the price for them, a weighty consideration to many of us, especially when we happen to live in the suburbs, or country, and have to wrestle with the dripping of the trees. Shop assistants invariably assure a purchaser over and over again, that the really good felts clean splendidly, and so they do, provided the tree drippings don't happen to come into the scheme. A New Embroidery.—" Maccaroni " embroidery is a novelty, and when carried out in a dull fabric, and applied to a bright one, or vice versa it is a decided success. As the name suggests, it is a worm-like piping, circular in shape, and sewn oil to form queer irres- ponsible looking patterns. Good Furs.—The fur problem is a difficult one to solve when one possesses tall ideas and little capital, as many of us have doubtless discovered. There are girls and women who because they cannot have the best, go without furs altogether, and perhaps in the end they are the wisest, for cheap imitation furs, like cheap imitation laces, represent really so much wasted money. It is better to save up for a little of the best, or go without entirely. So far, " faked " furs are little in evidence, bear having taken their place among the multitudes which is good hearing, as it shows that popular taste is improving. For the" furless," knitted chenille stoles,very much the shape of the clerical ones, are being offered. :Beautifully warm, and soft, with their long fringed ends weighting them, these stoles fall gracefully to well below the knees, and when matching exactly the costume over which they are worn prove exceedingly effective. Irish Industries.—The Irish fair held last week under the auspices of the Gaelic Society of London, illus- trated not only the remarkable progress of Irish farm- ing, but also of the many peasant industries and pro- ductions, which appeal to tho artistic femine mind. The beauty of Irish lace is familiar to all of us, and of which there were some splendid specimens from Carrickmacross and Limerick. But these cottage industries have extended in many other directions. There were for instance carpets of exquisite design, woollen railway rugs, splendid ones for 10s. or 125., fancy woodwork, and gypsum models. The display of hosiery and embroidery was also very attractive, and the varied assortment of pure woollens, and homespuns, was of general interest. It was the fifth annnal show initiated and managed by tho Society, and proved altogether more successful and a great advance on the previous ones. Women in the Working Trades.—Miss Meakin, the author of "Women in Transition," says that it would be hard to name a dozen callings that are now closed to women. Some point was given to this by attention having been drawn so the fact that a woman conducts and works at a regular carpenter's business in London, but there is nothing new in women being employed in the working trades. The records of that well-known church St. Martins in the Fields, near Charing C, oss, in London, show that payments were frequently made in olden times to women for structural repairs such as brickwork, ironwork, painting, glazing, and for even the casting. of a bell weighing over 7 cwt. Women were recognised members of the various guilds. and it would almost seem that they filled a larger place in the working trades than they do now, although of course it is only in recent times they have entered the professions.</p>
        <p>of - '• ,• • • i.;4‘</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>:eqt SELF—RAISING FLOUR. BtoEPS/ I 'SrITY .</head>
        <p>:eqt SELF—RAISING FLOUR. BtoEPS/ I 'SrITY .</p>
        <p>Women Councillors.—lt seems to have been too hastily taken for granted that the Act passed this year enables all women, whether married or single, to be elected county or municipal councillors. According to the " Law Times " this is not the case and it is only single women who are eligible to serve on the County and Borough Councils. All the Act does is to enable single women to be elected on these Councils,for which they had already the vote, but the position of married women remains the same. They can neither vote or serve on such bodies until a new Act is passed, and this it is to be understood is it to be introduced next session. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WHITE CITY, MANCHESTER.</head>
        <p>WHITE CITY, MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>From the time the visitor enters the gates of the White City, Manchester, until it is time for closing, the thousands of well pleased patrons find enjoyment in many forms and all the attractions offered there meet with full appreciation, which surely testifies to their great popularity. The best bands in Great Britain give their concerts afternoon and evening. In the magnificent ball room dancing reigns supreme and scores of happy dancers pursue this popular recreation on the splendid floor to tuneful music supplied by the White City Orchestra, under the direction of Mr. L. C. Everett. The skating rink and all other attractions have their followers. The Free Shows, such as the American Circus, Fairy Fountain, Illuminations, etc., are drawing large numbers, as there is plenty of varied entertainment. The management have much pleasure in announcing that they have secured for a two weeks engagement commencing Monday October 7th, the marvellous Zenobias " Kings of the Lofty Globe," marvels of the air, who will give two free exhibitions daily across the lake on a single telephone wire. These artistes give .an altogether incomprehensible original illustration of spherical genius. They are the only artistes in the world who propel a rolling globe on a high wire without the aid of balancing poles, and while the ball is being propelled by the one on the wire the other performs daring feats of strength and agility on a trapeze suspended from the globe. It is without doubt one of the greatest sensations ever intro- duced since the great Blondin—the act must be seen to be appreciated. Also the engagement of the wonderful Meteors, who will perform in the Circus a Comedy Double Trapeze Act. These, together with the numerous other free attractions will, make this pleasure resort well worth a visit.</p>
        <p>SAVED FROM DEATH! CHAS. FORBES Bile Beans Rescue a Salvationist. Indigestion, Amamia, Headaches, and</p>
        <p>_ Fainting Fits since Childhood. Solemn Declaration</p>
        <p>before a Commissioner for Oaths.</p>
        <p>No more wonderful proof of the power of CHAS. FORDE'S Bile Beans could be given than that supplied by Mrs. Lizzie Grantham, a salvationist, of 205, Stafford Street, Burton-on-Trent. Chas. Forde's thoroughly triumphed, in a few months, over complaints that had for years defied doctors, hospitals, and medicines, showing once again the immense superiority of Chas. Forde's original and genuine remedy over mineral pills and worth- less imitations which the great success of Chas. Forde's Bile Beans has called forth.</p>
        <p>To the "Burton Daily Mail " reporter Mrs. Grantham said :—" AU my life I suffered from anmmia and indigestion. When I was 18, I spent eight months in hospitals, but I derived no benefit whatever. For years I was subject to fainting fits, and often when out marching with the Salvation Army my comrades have had to carry me into neighbour's houses, or into Barracks, in a dead faint. The indigestion was so severe that I was afraid to eat, knowing full well what awful pains would follow. I was depressed and miserable, and I couldn't walk upstairs without resting on almost every step. I had awful pains across the chest, and was racked with chronic headaches.</p>
        <p>"In January, 1905, two doctors and a nurse were attending me. I was poulticed back and front. While I lay so ill, my sister gave me some Chas. Forde's.' When I had taken the first box I was easier. I put everything else on one side and persevered with Chas. Forde's,' and in a little time [ was able to leave what every- one thought would be my death-bed. My friends were astounded to see me up and looking so well again. Of course I continued with 'Chas. Forde's,' which gradually banished my complaints and eventually restored me to a state of health which I had never previously known. "For two and a half years now I have enjoyed sound health, which is what I never before possessed in my life. The fainting fits have entirely ceased, the indigestion and arilemia have left me, and I am feeling strong and vigorous. Only a short time ago I walked from Ashby-de-la- Zouch to ray home, a distance of ten miles, without a break, a thing that I should have thought madness to attempt before I took 'Chas. Forde's.' I am always recommending Chas. Forde's Bile Beans, but the best testimonial Chas. Forde's ' can ever have is for my friends to see me walking about—looking healthy and strong after being, to all appearances, a dying woman."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Grantham confirmed the whole of the above statements in a solemn Statutory Declar- ation, before Mr. Skinner, a Burton-on-Trent Commissioner for Oaths. Carefully note that it was Chas. Forde's that cured Mrs. Grantham's obstinate ailment. No cheap imitations or worth- less substitutes would have done this. Don't experiment with your health! See that you get " Chas. Forde's" the kind you have always bought.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>LOCOMOTION OF THE SNAIL.</head>
        <p>THE LOCOMOTION OF THE SNAIL.</p>
        <p>That great class of the mollusca to which the snail belongs obtains its name of Gas- teropoda from the fact that locomotion is effected by a flattened part of the ventral aspect of the body known as the foot. Exactly how the " foot " acts in the creep- ing of the gasteropod has been a matter of discussion. It has been explained as due to vibratile cilia on the foot, to flow of blood into its tissues, and to muscular contraction. In the Com ptes Rendus, MM. Dubois and Vies give an account of experiments they have carried out with a view of settling the question. As a result of their investigations, they reject the former two explanations in favour of the latter. The species experi- mented with was " Fissurella neglecta," but the conclusion may perhaps apply to all the gasteropoda.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Grappenhall Parish Church.</head>
        <p>Grappenhall Parish Church.</p>
        <p>There was a special service at this church on Saturday on the occasion of the unveiling of a memorial tablet to the late Rev. Richard Greenall, for many years rector of the parish. The ceremony was performed by the Archdeacon of Chester (the Ven. Archdeacon E. Barber) and there was a fair congregation. After unveiling the tablet, the Archdeacon delivered a short address. He said he had not merely been chosen as Archdeacon of the diocese to unveil the memorial, but as a close and intimate friend of the late rector. He paid a tribute to the good work done by the late Rev. Richard Greenall, who, he said, was well known throughout the diocese and he did everything in a true and religious spirit. The following is the inscription on the tablet : "In memory of the Rev. Richard Greenall, rector of this parish and honorary secretary of the Chester diocesan guild of church bell-ringers from 1894 to 1904. This tablet was placed by the members of the guild in grateful appreciation of the work he did for them." The tablet, which is of marble, has been placed at the west end of the church.</p>
      </div>
