        <p>to £110,680, and I think you will consider this satis- factory, especially if you knew, as I do, what care had been taken to make it a true representation of the profits of the company, by dealing with depreciation, removals, and taking most moderate views of the value of the stock. We keep as much as we possibly can for the profits of the succeeding year. Now in reference to the profits, it is a matter, of note that the ratio is in both iron and coal as nearly as possible in proportion to the capital employed. The capital of the iron and engineering trade is about 'double the capital of the coal trade, and the profits are in the same proportions. There is always a healthy spirit of emulation 1 etween our officials, and in this instance they have achieved equally satisfactory results.</p>
        <p>Mrs. E. PACK, 16, F Street, Bedford, writf "Sometime ago! contrao a verybad bronchialcougl, which made my chest very sore right down to the pit of the stomach. I could no sleep at night for the con, stant hacking cough, whicT often made me very sick, a: seemed to strain the wholf my body. I could scarce, breathe at times, and ' pain through the lu right to the back oi ;houlders was agonizing "Peps soon brought rnL wonderful relief from the very first. The cough became easier, and th, tablets loosened the phlegm and cleared the chet I could sleep well at night and the sickness an, lung pains were banished. I kept on taking th, Peps, and now the cough has disappearef altogether, and all pain has gone."</p>
        <p>Mrs. J. McCARTHY, 93, Shacklewell Lane, ID; London, N.E., writes :—" I am pleased to say I have I Peps to be amost valuable remedy for children's bror My little girl, aged three years, was constantly tr 4 with brrmichial colds, and we were astounded at th 4 wav</p>
        <p>per cent., and any further increase in wages would have made your dividend disappear altogether. Now you may not have it before you, but any of you who read the article in that very able weekly journal, .The Statist," on this company's accounts in last week's issue would see that the average dividend paid during the last ten years has only amounted to 31 per cant. per annum, including the dividend now paid, and there- fore, if we had been called upon to pay any larger increase in the wages than the amount which we have paid (and which was adjusted by a Conciliation Board as the fair amount to which the wage earner was entitled) during the year, your average dividend as shareholders would have been considerably less than 3i per cent. Now, speaking for myself, I consider that an extremely poor figure over a term of years, and not at all up to the return which ought to be earned upon capital in trading concerns, sabject to all the variations and vicissitudes of trade. I venture to think that we ought to claim a little sympathy from the Labour Party for that poor return, and except assistance from them in improving both the position of capital and labour in future years, instead of the querulous cry for a larger cdntribution out of our very moderate profits. (Hear, hear.) So much for the general figures, includ- ing coal and iron, but when we come to consider the coal trade, in respect of which the article of production is one of the prime necessities of life, I feel bound to make some further notes, as from the remarks in the Press and the pictures in " Punch " you would imagine that coal owners were growing very rapidly rich, and that undue profits —we are not soap millionaires in the iron and coal trade, nor do we earn our money so easily—were squeezed by them out of the pockets of consumers, and that labour is defrauded out of its proper proportion. I think the 31 per cent. average dividend during the last ten years tells a different tale. As a matter of course, it is the coal owners who are now being criticized as having effected the removal of the coal tax in order to feather their own nests, but I think, if you will refer to the Parliamentary proceedings, you will find that it was the doctrinaire exponents of Free Trade and also the miners' representatives who demanded the removal, as they stated it prevented the rise in wages. which was a necessity_for the miners, and could not be obtained owing to the coal tax. Well, there is no doubt that the removal of that tax has brought in a host of foreign customers for English coal, and has improved both the wages of the miners and the profit of the coal owners at the present time but when the blame is entirely placed upon the coal owner for the increase in the cost of ooal, it is forgett- ing the share and influence labour has on the counsels of the nations. (hear, hear.) The public consented to the miners' demand to abolish the coal tax, and are ready to give the collier increased wages, and they must not, therefore, be surprised if they have to pay higher prices for fuel. I would also like to add that the increased rate of wages means much less work (with larger wages) by the collier, Ivho gives less attention to his work. Ido not blame Aim ; in fact, I can refer to certain days of the week at our collieries when the attendance of the men does not exceed 59 per cent. of the number who ought to be in the pits. I don't think we have ever had so busy or so exacting a year, and I would only say that, as we all know, work is higher when it is leading to satisfactory results. As regards the future, I do not propose to prophesy. Matters look as if we should do better than last year, and we are well equipped to meet the calls for business which may be made upon us. The only thing one has to point out to shareholders is that the necessity for being up to. the highest standard of work frequently imposes upon a company the obligation of expending large capital sums in order to make their works perfect, and while we have nothing at present in contemplation, yet there are lines on which it may be necessary to take steps to further the productive power of this company in regard to iron from ,ore to the finished article. t would conclude by expressing my gratitude to our officials for the very able way in which they have con- ducted the business of the company. (Applause.) • Mr. W. H. BLECKLY seconded, and the motion was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>THE DIVIDEND.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN moved : "That the usual dividend at the rate of 6 per cent. per annum on the preference shares for the half-year ending 30th June last, and a dividend at the rate of 15 per cent. per annum, free of income tax, on the ordinary shares, be forthwith paid."</p>
        <p>had a large interest in the company, and that, combine 4 with his long connection with it, and his attachment t 4 it, made him endeavour to do his best to serve itl interests. (Applause.) RE-ELECTION OF AUDITORS.</p>
        <p>Mr. DAVID GARNETT moved: "That Messrs. Bleasi and Sons be re-appointed auditors for the ensuing yes at a salary of £300." Dr. BLAKEMORE seconded, and the motion was carried</p>
