      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>PROFIT ON COAL</head>
        <p>THE PROFIT ON COAL</p>
        <p>looms apparently very large in the eyes of the public. but it has not been so great as you might be led to imagine by the reports in the press. The profit is naturally based upon contracts made early in 1906, and there were periods during the time from Ist July, 1906, to 30th June, 1907, when the trade was by no means free from anxiety and depression. So much PO was this the case, that the average profit on coal made during the whole twelve months end- ing Ist July last, was only Bd. per ton, by no means an unreasonable figure. This low figure is, in view of public comments on the profits of the coal owner, startling, perhaps, but remember, there has been a great addition to the wages of colliers, and also the cost of stores, all of which has to be found out of any rise in the price of coal before the profit comes in to you. So far as regards the iron trade, the works have been con- tinuously busy, and there has been a good demand for foreign markets, as well as colonial markets,distinguish- ing this year from many previous years,when apparently the English iron trade was shut out from the foreign markets of the world. Ido not know to what we may attribute this, whether to the fact that our rivals have been too fully employed at home to seek out foreign markets, or whether it has arisen by greater ability on the part of English manufacturers to compete with foreign traders. For myself I think it is a mixture of both. Our engineering works have kept up a high reputation for their work, and we have been successful in obtain- ing and retaining orders for wheel centres with the new hydraulic press. We believe our finished wheels and axles on the new wheel'centres have given great satis- faction. We have completed a considerable contract for the Staveley Coal and Iron Company, Ltd., for blast furnaces, and the iron and steel work for the large sheds of Messrs. Carmel], Laird and Company at Sheffield and Birkenhead has been erected by us. We have also been successful in obtaining con- tracts for by-product coke ovens, gas producers and also large installations of colliery plant. I am pleased to say that the improvement of business has led to an advance in wages for the benefit of our work- men. Daring the year both at the collieries and iron works we have had to face the requirements of the new Compensation Act, and as most manufacturers antici- pated, the new clause which provides for compensation to date from the first day, provided the injury lasts one fortnight, has been most disturbing and injurious. Expression was strongly given in Parliament to the fact that it would be better to make an accident date from the first at all times sooner than adopt this miserabll arrangement, for in every case it is found that where an injury lasts a week it is by some means (we need not say what) extended to a fortnight, to bring it under the clause dating payment from the time of accident, end this, you can well understand, is most detrimental to the morale and efficiency of workmen. Your directors have truly had a very busy and anxious time during the past twelve months. You can well understand that when I tell you that our total sales for the twelve months including Rylands. but excluding the two new collieries, amounted to £1,990,247, and the total wages which we have paid during the same period, including Rylands' but excluding the two collieries, have amounted tO £488,390. (Applaase.) Now, as those figures will be fresh in your memory, having just mentioned them, I would like to refer to the amount of wages paid. We hear much said that</p>
        <p>LABOUR DOES NOT GET ITS FAIR SHARE OF PROFITS and this is pushed home so much by the Labour Party on the platform and their able representatives and in the Press, that it is desirable to enlighten our share- holders to some extent upon the real facts. Well, looking at the above figures, you cannot help seeing that a five per cent. rise in wages would have amounted if such rise bad been given, to £24,000, and that would have reduced your dividend for this year to under 6 per cent. If an increase of wages of 10 per cent. had been given it would have reduced your dividend to under 2</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>MR. J. J. BLECKLY AND TRADE DEVELOPMENT.</head>
        <p>MR. J. J. BLECKLY AND TRADE DEVELOPMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. J.BLECKLY said: In seconding this resolution I can re-echo what the Chairman has said—that the directors themselves ate thoroughly satisfied with the result of the year's working. They look upon it with the more satisfaction, because it is the direct outcome of the policy which they have maintained for so long, which I may perhaps describe as conservative finance and enterprising development of their trade. It is to this more than to any particular boom or any special advantages that the trade has presented that our thanks are due for the results that we have obtained to-day. In 1900 and 1901 we only used up in our own works two per cent, of the finished iron and steel that we menu- factured. That is, we were our own customers to that extent and trusted to the world for the rest of our orders. Last year, you will perhaps be surprised to , know, thirty per cent. of all the output of our mills and ' forges was used up in our own works in our engineering department and the thousand and one specialities that ; are the staples of our wire trade. You see the effect 1 of that is enormously beneficial. It steadies the demand, reduces the competition for orders outside, and tends to the economical working and regular employment of the men, which is the foundation of a successful business, especially in the iron trade. In 1900 and 1901 our trade with Itylands Bros., who were outsiders, and might have taken their orders away from us at any time was a very considerable one. It has increased by 44 per cent. in the six years we have been together, and now that trade represents to us something like £200,000 a year. It will be interesting to you to know that in 1901 the output of wire rods in this concern was 18,288 tons. Last year it was 29,279 tons, or an increase of sixty per cent. I think from the point of view of development we have also been successful, and that we may congratulate ourselves that that large operation , has resulted so much to the advantage of the company. I think the Chairman has perhaps answered sufficiently what now almost takes the form of an accusation against us—that we have had a boom in the iron trade and a boom in the coal trade. So far as we are concerned we have seen very little of it except in the papers. We have had fair trade and fair prices. Our prices have advanced ten or twelve per cent, on what they , were last year, but they are something like twenty aer cent. lower than in the boom of 1900 and , 1901, an I don't think we can recognize anything in I the shape of a boom in the iron trade stall. As regards the coal trade, the boom that represents a rise of sd. per ton on the average sales on a million ton of coal, won't carry you very far. Well, we are hoping for b4ter things in the coal trade this year, and have begun well in other directions. The better equipment of our works and the consolidation of our business should help us, and we mean to make the best of any trade that is to be had. The resolution was carried. RE-ELECTION OF A DIRECTOR.</p>
        <p>I Mr. W. P. RYLANDS moved: "That Mr. J. S. Harmood Banner, M.P. be re-elected a director of the company." No words of his, he said, were needed to commend the resolution to the favourable consideration of the meeting. The motion was carried. ! The CHAIRMAN, in reply, said that, as they knew, he</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>VOTES OF THANKS.</head>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he had already referred to thei, officials' work during the year, and he had, therefore. much pleasure in moving: "That the beat thanks of the meeting be given to the Secretary, Managers, ant other officers of the company at the iron works am colleries for their services during the past year.'' (Applause.) Mr. W. J. WALLINGTON sec onded, and the motion was carried with much heartiness. Mr. DODDS, Mr. J. KNOWLES, and Mr. C. E. BI7NOH responded. Mr. H. S. BLECKLY proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Banner for presiding. This motion was also carried.</p>
        <p>1 Cemetery Thefts. ! Mary Bowen, 7, Hale-street • Margaret Astil, 18, Halo-street; and Mary Whitaker, 56, Watkin-street, were summoned on Monday for committing damage to certain wreaths in the Cemetery on September 22nd,by taking ribbons. -Bowen and Astill pleaded guilty and Whittaker not guilty. Peter Bradford said he saw, the defendants standing near his mother's grave in the Cemetery,and he saw one of them put something in her pocket.He went to the grave and found that a ribbon had gone. He thought Whittaker was innocent, because she seemed to want to get away from the others He went to the girls and found the piece of ribbon (produced) in Bowen's pocket. -Mr. Young, registrar at the Cemetery, said it was an old complaint about people coming to the Cemetery and taking ribbons from wreaths.—Defendants had nothing to say.—Whittaker was dismissed and Bowen and Astill were fined is.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>MEDICINAL TREASURES OF ANCIENT CIVILISATION.</head>
        <p>MEDICINAL TREASURES OF ANCIENT CIVILISATION.</p>
        <p>A Wonderful Remedy Revealed.</p>
        <p>" KER-NAK Soothes while it Cures."</p>
        <p>The Persians, when the ruling nation of the East, made 'a fashion of fruit eating. Their con- quered subjects, the Egyptians, were, however, the first to learn the art of converting certain valuable proportions of fruit into potent medicines. Through the Egyptians, the medicinal value of fruit became known to the Greeks mid Romans, and the art eventually reached Great Britain as a carefully-guarded secret of the Italian monks. Twentieth century science has made great strides in the direction of Natural medicines. There is now brought vtithin the reach of all the most wonderful medicine of all—Ker-Nak which is compressed into the handy form of a small pill, and is pronounced by all who have tried it to be the most natural and most potent remedy for liver, stomach and bowel troubles. Ker-Nak rises superior to the old-fashioned drastic remedies in that it contains no unnaturally searching drugs and minerals, und not only cures an ailment, but is a protection against its re-appearance. . .</p>
        <p>The timely use of Ker-Nak may be depended upon to rescue a person from the clutches of one or other of the disastrous diseases of the liver, stomach, and bowels. Ker-Nak, indeed, leaves old-fashioned mineral pills far in the background. In its concentrated pill form, Ker-Nak contains certain essences and oils, the valuable constituents of fresh, ripe fruit in association with wholesome vegetable extracts. Thus Ker-Nak is a compact medicine, a natural and ideal remedy, possessing the laxative and curative equivalent of many fruits rolled into one.</p>
        <p>er-Nak alleviates and cheeks disease in a soothing and natural way. Cheap and nauseous mineral drugs are thus entirely superseded by Ker-Nak.</p>
        <p>_ To understand the /enormous superiority of Ker-Nak over old-fashioned remedies, one has but to call to mind the manner in which drastic drugs act, "scouring "- and injuring, the membrane or delicate lining of the bowels, frequently paralysing the muscles which lie beneath the lining, and thus weakening, when not entirely suspending the natural action of the organs.</p>
        <p>Ker-Nak is unique in that it soothes and comforts thc organs while ridding them of their trouble, and thus it builds up the membrane, which old remedies tend to destroy. Ker-Nak, as it were, lifts the patient into a sound, healthy condition. The most obstinate and long-standing symptoms of liver, stomach, and bowel troubles disappear under the potent and soothing influence that is stored up in the email compass of a Ker-Nak pill. As a family medicine without equal, Ker-Nak is already widely reconised. N little case of Ker-Nak is a veritable medicine chest in miniature. Kept ever at hand, ready to resort to the moment one has that out-of sorts feeling or a sense of sickliness, Ker-Nak will saVe you many pounds in doctors' bills. Ker-Nak is not only a remedy La., but a pro- tection against constipation, indigestiou, bilious attacks, headache, nausea, flatulence or wind, depression, anitmia, feminine ailments, pale, sallow, and sickly complexions, sleeplessness, palpitation, aches and pains in the back and loins, shortness of breath, pimples, blood impurities, heartburn, loss of appetite, bad breath, piles, chill on the liver or kidneys causing backache; and other prevalent liver, stomach and bowel troubles, rheumatic gout, influenza and its after-effects, and the many ailments developed by the cold weather. ,Sold by all chemists and stores, aad by the sole proprietors, Ker-Nak Natural Remedy, Ltd., Turnmill St., London, E.C., in is. 11d. and 2s. 9d. cases. Ker-Nak is ge9tle yet thorough; it soothes while it cures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>BURIED IN THE SAND.</head>
        <p>BURIED IN THE SAND.</p>
        <p>Johh Bickford, an ex-soldier, was buried by a fall of sand in a pit at Headley, Hants, on Monday. A rescue party set to work to dig him out, but when discovered Bickford was dead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WOMAN'S REALM.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>QtEEN OF SPAIN'S DOLL.</p>
        <p>The .Queen of Spain, when Princess Ena of Battenberg, shewed her interest in poor girls by the patronage she extended to the Girl's Recans Guild, which has a fund for helping those of gentle birth by training them in some suitable way to earn their own living. The funds are gained by members of the guild, chiefly by means of a great bazaar. She has sent a magni- ficent doll, dressed as a lady of Spain, to be sold for the trust fund at the next bazaar, to be held in the Queen's Hall in December.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>SIMPLE AND PRETTY FROCK</head>
        <p>A SIMPLE AND PRETTY FROCK</p>
        <p>Very practical and useful, as well as modish and becoming, is the little frock pictured in our sketch. Its simplicity makes it just the thing for the business girl's daily wear, for every-day afternoon use at home, or, indeed, for any but a really dressy occasion. The material in which it is realised is a light-weight serge, in colour a warm deep brown; but any pretty woollen fabric, such as cashmere, frieze, or cloth, would serve equally well for the purpose. There is just a hint of the pinafore corsage in the way in which the bodice is hollowed out at the neck to shew a dainty little vest of lace. This vest, by the way, is easily detachable for washing pur- poses and may be made in lawn, muslin, or net instead of in lace if preferred. Round the top of the bodice comes a narrow band of the mate- rial, which is firmly machined on either side. The</p>
        <p>bodice itself is arranged in a series of box-pleats, one being placed in the middle of both front and back and two on either side of it. These pleats are machined on either side for part of their length, and then released, the three middle pleats being further adorned by small embroi- dered buttons. Beyond the outermost pleat pro- jects a jaunty little epaulette, which gives to the front the correct sloping shoulder. The sleeves are of the newest type, quite long, and cut very much after the fashion of the gigot sleeve which was so popular some time ago. As for the skirt, it, too, is quite simple, being made to fit accu- rately over the hips, but cut with a box-pleat back and front, these pleats being machined and buttoned to match the corsage. A wide hem machined at the edge and finished with a row of buttons completes this smart and useful gown.</p>
        <p>.0 ;Is ,r</p>
