      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>GIRDLE OF AMARYLLIS.</head>
        <p>THE GIRDLE OF AMARYLLIS.</p>
        <p>The girdle of the modern nymph is represen- ted by a stout leather belt, sometimes studded with nails, always uncompromisingly utilitarian- looking, and it is this weapon of offence that we are asked to believe is going to be dragged into a seventeen-inch circumference. Personally, writes Ella Hepworth Dixon in the Sketch, I put about as much credence in these alarmist rumours (which occur with pleasing regularity) as I do in those picturesque accounts of the sea- serpent which endear to us the correspondence in the morning papers at this autumnal season. For we are always being threatened with the wasp-waist, but, except in the case of a few fool- ish women of all ages, it somehow never "ar- rives." . The fact is that the exaggeratedly tiny belt looks second-rate, and suggests at once the smart shop-girl and the mannequin; it is, more- over, never seen on any of the famous "good dressers "—and their numbers are nowadays many—in English society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>CHARMING BLOUSE.</head>
        <p>A CHARMING BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>Very up-to-date in style and typical of present fashions is the charming blouse illustrated in the sketch. Dark green velveteen, -exactly matching in colour the skirt with which it is worn, is the material in which it is realised, the velveteen being of good quality and thin, supple wet:ire. The fronts and back are treated exactly alike, the velvet being arranged in two enor- mcusly wide folds on either side of a V-shaped</p>
        <p>opening, which comes down to the waist.. In this opening is displayed a dainty vest of imita- tion huh crochet of a rather deep ivory tone, which is finished a couple .of inches below the neck by a little round strapping of satin exactly matching the velvet in colour. Above this strapping comes an inner chemisette and neck- band of finely-tucked ivory net, patterned with a small spot. The sleeves are in the latest style, long enough to come well down on to the hands, rather full at the top, but fitting closely to the am below the elbow, and finished with a row of embroidered buttons at the back. ENGLISH MATRONS FOR AMERICAN HOSPITALS. Miss Lucy Walker has retired from the post of superintendent nurses at the Pennsylvania Hospital, Philadelphia, and her successor, Miss Emily Payne, has entered upon her duties. Both Miss Walker and Miss Payne were trained in Great Britain, the former at St. Bartholomew's Hospital, London, and the latter at the Royal Infirmary, Edinburgh. Miss Payne, who has re- cently been at the head of the Episcopal Hos- pital in Philadelphia, was for five years Miss Walker's assistant.</p>
        <p>.‘ 1 -'":', an c&amp;.- -7 r! • \ •'.' r !I • ----,----,-. I • - - , N4:'\'\ . \• \ -\:‘, / ,:,• ' , ‘lk. . • :i (1 i''' .(.\\,• \\9\ „li - ' Ti e.' •Nii.,__lii .. 7 I . \ ---'1( F,l / .1 'lli ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>13LACEBERRY BLANCMANGE. - Pick over the blackberries carefully so as to remove any stalks or leaves, and measure out two breakfastoup- fuls. Place in a saucepan on the fire with a quart of water, and allow to boil very slowly for about ten or twelve minutes. In the meantime make a paste of four, level tablespoonfuls of cornflour with a little cold water, and, when smooth, stir the cornflour into the stewed blackberries, work- ing all rapidly over the fire until the mixture thickens, and taking care to avoid lumps. Then remove_ continue stirring for a few moments. adding summent castor sugar to sweeten. Your into a mould previously rinsed out in cold water, and set aside to cool. When firm and cold, turn out, and cover with thick cream Savoured pith little lemon,iuices</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>MESSRS. PEARSON AND KNOWLES LTD.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. PEARSON AND KNOWLES LTD.</p>
        <p>34th Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>SPEECH BY MR. HARMOOD BANNER, M.P.</p>
        <p>Tile 34th annual meeting of the above company was held -Itt the offices, Bewsey-road, on Monday, at 12 noon, "for .„.0 Purpose of receiving and passing the accounts for Year ending June 30th last, and for transacting the Eeneral business of the company." Mr. J. S. Harmood asilrner, M.P. (chairman of the Board), presided, and e were also present :—Mr. William H. Bleckly, VePutv-chairman). Mr. John H. Bleckly, Mr. Richard lick.er, Mr. W. Peter Rylands, and Mr. El. S. Bleckly, Iheetors'; !sir. James Dodds (secretary), and several 'llsreholders. The report of the directors and the statement of 41,0,... omits, which have already appeared in our columns, "re taken as read. CHAIRMAN'S SPEECH. INTERESTING REVIEW OF "HE COAL AND IRON TRADES. rlhe CFIAIRMAN, before moving the adoption of the 7Port and the statement of accounts, said :—I am sure leo will expect me to ask you to join with me in Vclaressing the regret the directors feel at the loss of reir late colleague, Mr. George PO3arson. We cannot Vok hack upon the company without one regret, and at is that we have not a director of the name of nterirson on our Board. (Hear, hear.)—Proceeding. the I'l-ml4n:tan said: In moving the adoption of the report tlild statement of accounts, I am sure you will appreciate ee satisfaction with which 1 now present them to the ~clareholders of the company. The profits are satis- ,.*Setely and the balance sheet is an exceedingly sound re; and I believe the cautious dealing with the profits, ITsides the very excellent dividend which we have Tes.tributed, will commend itself to you. Now before thlan,g into the figures I think it right to inform you be satisfactory as this balance sheet is, it would have onr"yossible to place in a still more satisfactory way unancial position before you. As you would .17"rve from , the report, the purchase of the res of Messrs. Rylands Brothers, Limited, has II"? been completed, and the whole of their ulasiness is in the hands of this company, and Joint balance sheet amalgamating the assets, liabilities, Profits, and reserves of Messrs. Rylands Bros. Ltd., the assets, liabilities, profits and reserves of Messrs. rearson and Knowles, instead of the mere holding of .thkares, would have shown a much stronger position the statement for the year to 30th June, 1907, Vilch is now submitted. As regards the shares in the -,1-13,ss Hall and Wigan Junction Collieries, they were acquired in March of this year, but fortunately t 6 condition of the coal trade has made the purchase Iteneficial to this company, and we are already corn- out of the profits to reduce the liabilities we had to undertake when purchasing the two _ollieries.Both collieries contain a good supply of cannel ivhieh is becoming exceedingly scarce in the neighbour- which° is of considerable value for gas-making °sea. The directors have been thoroughly satisfied 1a.,‘0 the results of the purchase of these collieries. the liability side of the account you will find a item of bonds redeemable, at two, three, and five ars, £108,575. These are not debenture bonds, and ,eate no charge upon the company's assets, and such, ,ass the credit of the company that I think I may say re directors had only to mention the fact of their "ipirements and the money was subscribed at once. hale money was required for the purpose of paying the tance of the amount due for the preference shares of tot.sods Bros., and also towards the purchase money an the shares and debentures of the Moss Hall Company Thcl the shares of the Wigan Junction Colliery Company. 11,41 bonds bear interest at 5 per cent. The creditors 'front to £80,136, against £67,616 last year. e reserve fund was increased last year, and i.,, again to hi raised by the addition this year Z 150,000, add is more than represented by the o":eatinents on the reserve fund account, which appear Sr the asset side. I would repeat that the investments 1: all of greater value than the amount at which they 'rid hi the balance sheet. The profit this year amounts</p>
