      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. , The Chairman ar.d the Deputation.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. , The Chairman ar.d the Deputation.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, Mr. W. Houghton presiding. There were also present :—Mrs. Wilkinson, Mrs. Simpson, Rev. M. Ryan, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, W. Gill, S. Davies, W. Furness, A. Brooks, J. Backhouse, J. Cook, G. Mather, M. W. Seton, W. C. Brooks, R. Bolton, J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Awbery (Workhouse master) and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate Cottage Homes).</p>
        <p>_ RATABLE VALUES. 1 Mr. H. LAWRENCE submitted the new supplementary i valuation list for Warrington which showed an increase ! of ratable value of £3484 10s, gross and £2902 ratable. • The total ratable value of Warrington is now £329,548 10s. gross, £265,727 ratable. The increase, which is chiefly on house and cottage property, is slightly above the average.</p>
        <p>Mr.JOSEPH,SMITH reported that the ratable value of Sankey was 42495 on agricultural land and £B2BB on buildings &amp;c. This represented a total increase of 1112 10s.</p>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S BEREAVEMENT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Furrcßorr, the resident medical officer, wrote stating that his father died on Tuesday, and asking for a few days leave of absence.</p>
        <p>The request was granted. THE CHAIRMAN AND THE DEPUTATION. AN "UNCONSCIONABLE NUMBER."</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN made some strong comments with regard to the deputation from the Board to the Poor Law Conference held at Liverpool last week. He did not object to the scale on which the expenses were charged, but he disagreed with the constitution of the com- mittee. At the last meeting live were appointed and that, from a Board of 28, was an abnormal and unreason- able number. Therefore he refrained from joining the deputation. If he had gone he should have paid his own expenses. He would not be one of a batch of five, even if the Union were larger, because the deputation was unreasonably large. The CLERK: Only four were appointed. The CHAIRMAN: There were five—the Chairman Vice-chairman, Mr. Jolley, Mr. Mather and yourself. It is an unconscionable number to send. I am not finding fault with Mr. Jolley going, because he did , honour and credit to the Board by reading a paper. That I was a case where a point might be stretched, but I object to a deputation of that nuMber being appointed to visit that Conference or any other. I am a great I upholder of Conferences. I think they are the life and ' soul of Poor Law administration, but I don't think they ought to be abused and it would have been in ' this case, if five members had attended. I made up my mind from the first moment that I would not be one. Mr. Bovrox : I cannot see what value Mr. Jolley's paner has been to the Warrington Board and to the ratepayers. The CHAIRMAN: Some members of the Conference would see great benefit in it. You administer in your own way and other Unions administer in their's. There is a great deal of information in the paper foe Boards who have been lax in their conduct on that particular subject.._</p>
        <p>Mr. BOLTON said he thought they would have had some saving in their organization and he was greatly against the expenses being paid for the reading of a paper for the benefit of the Conference. The Warring- ton ratepayers should not have to besr the coat. The CLERK: Mr. Mather was included as Chairman of the Padgate Schools Committee. If four members had gone I could only have allowed the expenses for three.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: I should net have signed the cheque for five.</p>
        <p>The CLERIC said that when the deputation was appointed he had a suspicion that the Rev. T. IL Matthews,the vice-chairman, would not attend as he was not present at the preliminary meeting at Manchester. Miss Broadbent, Mrs. Simpson and Mr. A. Brooks went to the conference at their own expense. The CHAIRMAN: And they are the people above all others who should have had their expenses paid, when they volunteered to go in that way. If every Board of Guardians sent five, St. George's Hall would not accom- modate them, nor would Hengler's Circus. (Laughter). Mr. MATHER : I believe Dutton, a smaller Union, sent more delegates. _</p>
        <p>The discussion then ended. IMPROVED DIETARY SCALE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the House Committee on September 20th, the Dietary Committee after careful inquiry and investigation reported that they were unanimously of the opinion that a new scale of diet should be adopted for the chief indoor officers. The committee therefore recommended that a new dietary be adopted and snb- stituted for that now in force, the same to come into operation on Sunday, October 6th. The following tenders were accepted for six months: —Leather, Thomas Samuel and Co., Liverpool; tailors' goods and drapery, T.J. Lee; meat, H.Singleton; coffins A. Mason; brushes, George Ratcliffe ; fish, R. Melbuish' Grimsby ; clogs, 'William Jones; groceries, H. Milling and Co. wines and spirits, R.Rowson and Sons: boots, Messrs. Portctr, Liverpool; coal and slack, J. Pendle- bury and Co.; flour and oatmeal, James Hallwood. The minutes were approved. THE SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Cottage Homes Committee, it was recommended that the Clerk write to the Secretary of the Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children requesting that he will in future instruct their inspector in all cases in which it is desired to place children under the care of the Guardians that the admission shall be made on a relieving officers' order The following tenders were accepted :—Leather, Mr. F. Morley; milk, Mr. J. Houghton; brushes. Mr. G. Ratcliffe; bread, Mr. Eckersley ; dour, Mr.J. Hallwood; fuel, the Wigan Coal and Iron Company; groceries, Messrs. H. Milling and Co.; meat, Mr. H. Singleton; tailors' goods and drapery, dr. T. J. Lee. Mr. %V. D. Jeans, local secretary to the Society, wrote in. reply to the above communication stating that he had been informed by Inspector Cox that it had always been his practice to obtain a relieving officer's order for the admission of children to the Workhouse except when acting under Section 3 of the Cruelty to Children Act, 1904, which authorised the admission of children to the Workhouse as a place of salty on a justice's order under which the Workhouse Master was bound to receive the child or children the subject of such order. The inspector's instructions were to comply with all rules and regula- tions of the Guardians and other public bodies with whom he might be brought into contact, to avoid any possibility of conflict with public officials, and especi- ally they desired as a Society for harmonious relations with the Union officials, as from them they received so much highly valued assistance. - The minutes were passed. THE PROPOSED NEW OFFICES.</p>
        <p>The Board then went into committee to consider the question of the proposed new offices, Mr. Seger Owen attending to submit plans.</p>
      </div>
