      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>EXA</head>
        <p>THE EXA</p>
        <p>OCTOBER sth, 1907.</p>
        <p>Calling their opponents by SOCIALISM AND opprobrious names is a TARIFF REFORM. characteristic of the Con-</p>
        <p>servative Party. As an electioneering weapon it is not to be despised, and signs are already apparent that, having seen that Tariff Raform is not likely to win the people back to their party, members of the present Opposition hope at the next election to scare the people into supporting them by calling their opponents Socialists. Captain JESSEL, of the London Municipal Society, found these tactics 40 effective at the last London County Council election that they are now considering whether they cannot be applied to the whole country ; and, completely ignoring the real truth they will dub every supporter of the present Government a Radical-Socialist.Social reform is a vastly different thing from Socialisrn,but that makes no difference to these people. According to them, W 3 shall all be Socialists at the next election, and their paid street-corner agitators who, we are told, are now " cramming " Socialism will not hesitate to tell the people that Socialism means atheism, means free love, and means an end to private property. It is the same game that was played in 1900, when we were all "Little Englanders," "lovers of every country but our own," "Pro-Boers," and so on. It is an attempt to secure the abolition of our present Fiscal system on an entirely false issue. Let it be successful, and we shall see exactly the same sort of thing we saw in the Parliament of 1900-1905, when the Unionist Party, haYing got a renewed lease of power, proceeded to legislate on lines which had they but appeared in their election addresses, would never have been approved by the country. These people know that the publicimemoryl is short, but there are many of us who do not forget the Licensing and Education Acts of the last Parliament, measures which nobody would ever have dared to put in an election programme. Mr. BALFOUR had no House of Lords to tell him that the House of Commons did not represent the people. He was complacently obeyed. And so it will be again if the electors can only be frightened by the word "Socialism"ginto voting for the Unionist candidates. Those who are now divided regarding the merits of Tariff Reform are being asked to close their ranks to fight " Socialism, " but let them be successful, and which section of them would force the pace in the next Parliament ? The Protectionists would capture the whole party, and there would be an end to that Fiscal system which has made the prosperity of this country. Aecording to this same society which is organising the " attack on Socialism," the fight in London last March was a " fight for lower rates." Those who were in power were "bankrupting London," "looting the workers," and so on. Now they have been six months in power, and the announcement is made that they deprecate the idea that they promised to reduce the rates during their term of office. If they can only be as "efficient and progressive" as their predecessors and not put the rates up they will be satisfied. Yet these very predecessors last March were "Socialists." The trick will probably not succeed in London again; but it is just as well, when the same people are suggesting the extension of their methods to the whole country, to recall their promises and compare them with the performance. It will but amuse people in the country to be reminded that in London municipal trams are 'Socialism," that there are many London boroughs without a free library because of "Socialism," that municipal gas and water supplies are " Socialism," and that to suggest the municipal management of electric power is pretty well highway robbery. Most of these things are matters which in the provinces all parties agree are best in the hands of the community, and it is I hardly likely that the same cry will be as effective in the country as it was in the metropolis. All the same, it is well to be forewarned. If elec- tioneering is to be conducted at the next General, Election on the same lines as was the case in London last March, it will be necessary for those , who care for honesty and straightforwardness in public affairs to be ready when the time comes to expose the game that is being played upon them.</p>
        <p>The new mayor is the THE Nnw MAYOR. old mayor. Alderman</p>
        <p>Smethurst- has acceded to the unanimous wish of the Council and agreed to accept office for a second year. We are sure the townspeople will be gratified by his decision, for Alderman Smethurst has, during the past twelve months, proved himself a capital chief citizen. No ta3k has been too arduous for him, and we should think he has almost established a record in his devotion to public duty. As Chairman of the Council he has shown both tact and firmness, in addition to conspicuous impar- tiality, and by his efforts and example has done a great deal towards welding together in a work- able whole, a number of discordant units. A year ago, the Council Chamber was a sort of monthly bear-garden. To-day it approaches more nearly than for a long time past the semblance of a decorous public legislative asembly.</p>
        <p>The ratepayers are indebted A Smoua to Mr. E. England, one of ()DAEMON. the borough elective audi- _</p>
        <p>tors, for ifie trouble be has. taken in issuing a public report distinct from that submitted officially to the Council from the three auditors conjointly. There are usually plenty of things to which a conscientious auditor may reasonably draw public attention on his own account, after certifying that the books of the Cor- poration are admirably kept, and that two and two again add up to four. In his second annual report to the ratepayers—a copy of which is printed in another column,—Mr. England has some strong remarks to make about the health of the town. He quotes statistics to show that our death- rate trom all causes, from epidemic diseases, and infant mortality, is the worst on record when compared With nearly all the other towns in the kingdom. "There is," he says bluntly, "no getting over the fact that Warrington is one of the unhealthiest towns in England." We have no doubt that many residents in Warrington will dissent vigorously from such a statement, but they do not live in those quarters which do so much towards swelling the returns- The figures have certainly a significance which there is no avoiding. Mr. England points to the cause as lying in the insanitary conditions in which the people live—too much slum property, houses shut out from the sunlight and air and without the ordinary conveniences of civilization. He refers to the reports of the late Dr. Gornall and Dr. Gaffikin in support of his statements and charges the Council with liming in a fool's paradise where low rates are aimed at in preference to a low death-rate. The indictment is certainly a severe one, and should , set the Health Committee furiously to think , Warrington may show splendid returns from its I municipal trading ventures, but it cannot claim to a well-governed and enlightened municipality as long as it beats the record with its mortality returns. The evil is probably deep-seated and difficult to remedy, but in the directions indicated by the critic more activity is called for. Slain areas are plague spots,and they are not eradicated with the promptness and firmness that they should be in Warrington. Possibly our over-loaded sewerage system is another cause contributing to the unsatisfactory state of things. At any rate there is a problem here pressing tor solution with greater urgency than th 3 provision of new muni- cipal offices. A public abattoir also claims preference to the latter recently discussed scheme, for it has a direct bearing on the public health question.</p>
        <p>We should think the members of the Health Committee will feel their responsibilities pressing heavily this week-end, for Mr. England is not alone in his criticisms. At the Beautiful Warrington Society's annual meeting on Thursday evening, Mr. C. E. Parker and Alderman Bennett both said unflattering things about the Health Authority. Their theme was the oft-derided one of smoke. We are glad to see that Alderman Bennett has found a backer at last in the person of Mr. Parker. The former openly charged the Health Committee with culpable apathy, and the latter was insistent in his demand for more rigorous measures of smoke abatement. The nuisance at last bids fair to obtain serious recog- nition, for public opinion is awakening to the needlessness of the smoke pall, and to the fact that the law provides certain remedial measures. If offending manufacturers were brought to book oftener they would be more inclined to experiment with scientific stoking and combustion than they are to-day. The way of the transgressor is too easy in Warrington!</p>
        <p>No one could honestly say THE that the Town Council was TCWN COUNCIL. sewn at its best on Tuesday. It had nothing of great import to occupy its attention, so the consequence was that a number of mighty atoms were dissected and pulled to pieces by debate with a persistence worthy of a better cause. Principally, the discus- sions raged round baths, bowls, barbers and binoculars. It took a long time to decide what was to be done with the vacant land behind Garven Place, and even at the end of the discus sion no decision was come to. It will take another month to evolve anything definite. The Parka and Baths Committe desire to extend the baths and make a bowling green there. The Finance Committee does not object, but loftily says before you do this we will see if it would not be possible to build new Municipal offices here Possibly it would, but we do not think the Council will sanction the erection if another "Palace "—it would be a third would it not ? just yet. The fighting factions pulled the plot of land to pieces, metaphorically speaking, and finally the resolutions were referred back. In the meantime we believe the Borough Surveyor has to report on the amount of land available, and the facilities for making baths and a bowling green. The barbers are to have au order defining the hours at which their shops must close on each day of the week, and on Bank Holidays aud the like.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>NOTES. AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES. AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Probably if a vote were taken in the House of Commons as to who was its most popular member, and if the Speaker were excluded, the • great majority would put a mark against the name of Mr. Thomas Burt.— British Weekly." * * *</p>
        <p>The Borough Surveyor's Department has been authorized to purchase a pair of binoculars to " sea through"mid not to take to the Isle of Alan,as Alderman Tinnion explpined in answer to questions. We hope some of the inequalities in the road-surface will be seen with a clearer vision.</p>
        <p>* 0 elf We wonder if it has been realised in Warrington that ladies are now eligible for election to the borough Council. Now is the moment for the women suffragists who are on the burgess roll to come forward and challenge the men. it is also permissible to have a Mayoress without a Mayor * * _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Since Henry George -wrote- 'Progress and Poverty no one has any excuse for not knowing how the Labour Problem can be solved; :how to raise wages, cheapen goods, and lower rent, without doing the slightest injustice to a single human being.' —Mr. Evans, of Wellington, New Zealand, in "An Open Letter to the Minister for Lands."</p>
        <p>Among the distinguishred sons that Warrington has given to the country during the present generation (says the London correspondent of the "Manchester Courier ") are three whose careers, though widely different, are equally remarkable. Sir Luke Fildes, R.A., is one, and Mr. John Rymer Young and Mr. Alfred Harry Young are the others. All three were born in Warrington and educated at the same school. Fame came to the Royal Academician before the pharmacist and the anatomist attained the distinction which now plrices them in the front rank of men of science. Messrs. Young are the sons of a pharmacu- tical chemist whose place of business was for many years at the corner of Scotland-road and afterwards in Sankey-street. Mr. Alfred Young is the Pro-Vice Chancellor of Victoria University of Manchester, and is the leading anatomical authority in this country , Mr. John Young is the, President of the Pharma- ceutical Society, and, in that capacity, he on Tuesday presented the Hanbury Gold Medal to Mr. David Hooper, Curator of the Economic and Art Sections of the Indian Museum, Calcutta. Mr. Jolla Young has not left his native town, but has sprung, as it were, to the position he now occupies from his father's shop. My recollection of the President date. back many years. There is one incident in his life which I hope I may be forgiven for mentioning. la Warrington, as elsewhere, some three decades es.o, Parliamentary discussion societies were much to. the fore. At that time the writer was the Prime Minister and Mr. John Young held a Portfolio in his Administra- tion, while Mr. Alfred Young was one of his supporters</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <p>id</p>
        <p>In connection with the autumnal meetirtgs of the Temporary Authority. 'l3aptist Union held in Liverpool this week, the Rev. At the Borough Police Court, William Hankinsou Herbert Dunnico, as chairman of the Hospitality Cora- was granted temporary authority to sell at the Marquis mittee, has, had a gigantic task in providing accommo- of Granby Inn in place of Mr. F. J. Whittle —Mr. dation for 1,400 of the 1,900 delegates. Be has, how- Steel said applicant had been employed at Messrs. ever, been greatly assisted in his work by the ready Rylands for a number of years, and satisfactory testis monials had been handed to the Chief Constable. response of the various churches and is clelighted b the attitude of the Anglican clergy, many of whom. Joseph Warburton Robinson was granted temporary including the Bishop of Liverpool, have invited authority so sell at an off-licensed house in Legh-street delegates into their homes. Mr. Donnie° is the minister in place of Mr. Whitfield. of Kensington Church, and is well known throughout I PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST the city. BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. The Redhill at hoax the other day may be the Motor Engine on the Footpath, distant echo of a famous practical joke played on the .tL the Borough Court on Monday, Thomas Stacey, went to St. Helena a certain man gave out that the folk of Chester nearly a century ago. When Napoleon A alvuepoo-road by driving a heavy motor engine est er, was summoned for damaging the footpath island was over-run by rats, and an offer was made to m L across l it—Defendant_ did not appear.—P.C. Woods buy Cheshire cats for export. Scores of people with said defendant drove the engine across Cie footpath to hundreds of cats congregated in Chester on the day advertised. But no buyer turned up, and most of the get the Coach et to he and Horses Inn, instead of going the Cats were allowed to run loose. The damage they nrinper way. When he told defendant about it he said he oct not care; th