es and news" several stealing a pair of boots value 2s. 6d.; from the stall items that amuse us, not being stamp-collectors our- of William Sterling, in the Market at 8.30 on Saturday night.—Prisoner pleaded guilty. P.C. Gilchrist said selves. For instance a quotation from the "Australian Philatelist " records solemnly the fact that owing to he saw prisoner go into the• Market Place and he a Machinery mishap some of the Commonwealth (witness) followed him, and saw him take a--pair of stamps have been issued with only 111 perforations boots from the stall and put them under his coat in single line ins Prisoner then went away, and witness arrested him instead of 12. Several values have 111 all round while some actually have 11 with 111 corn- and took him back to the stall where Mr. Sterling pound. We don't know how exciting this may be for identified the boots as his property. —The Chief the inveterate stamp-hunter but it strikes our Constable said that prisoner had twelve previous% con- victions against him. —Prisoner was sentenced to a ignorant mind as being a somewhat humorous news month hard labour. item. A Ferocious Dog. Edward Lloyd, 25, Wakefi.eld-s'reet, was summoned at the Borough Court on Monday for keeping a ferocious dog.—Defeudant said the dog was not ferocious. C-irbett said he was going past defendant's back gate in Guardian-street, when a large dog rushed out and got hold of his trousers and tore them. Witness told defendant about it, but he took no notice and seemed very indifferent. —Defendant said the dog was not ferocious. —The Chief Constable said that if the defendant had not been so it, different the summons would not have been iasued. —Defendant was mulcted in the costs (78.) SPLENDID PURE BuTTRR,IO(I. per lb.at ASHLEY'S.—Adv More Street Trading Prosecutions. At the Borough Court on Monday before the Mayor and other magistrates Michael Donnelion(l2),3, Back Haydock-street, was charged with street trading with- out his badge on September 21st. Defendant pleaded guilty. P.C. Reddington said he saw defendant trading without his badge on and when asked for his name and address he gave the wrong one. The constable said it took him three days to find where defendant lived. Defendant was fined a shilling. William Henry Ellison (15), 43, Hamilton-street, was charged for a similar offence. P.C. Reddington said the boy had given his qence up about a week ago. He was ordered to pay 2s. 6d. William Leslie Lowe (11), 38, Foundry-street, was fined a shilling for trading in a like manner on September 21st in Sankey-street. PETROLEUM OIL IN BARRELS and Drums delivered free. Ask prices.—J. S. GARLAND, iron- monger, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Man Killed at Blackpool. A sad fatality occurred on the Blackpool Steam Packet Company's boat the Queen of the North, at the jetty of the Central Pier, Blackpool, on Saturday night. The steamer had returned from taking a large number of passengers to view the new Cunarder, Lusitania, in the Mersey, and the crew were removing stores, &amp;c., from the boat to the jetty. The boatswain, Peter Chantler(s7), a widower, who is believed to he a native of Warrington, was walking along the outside of the gangway holding on to the rails, when suddenly the gangway turned over and Chantler was precipi- tated into the water. He must have atm,* the sponsor of the boat, for after ejaculating "Oh," nothing more was heard of him. He was seen lying across the girders of the pier and was brought up in an uncon- scious condition. Artificial respiration was resorted to for over an hour, but when the doctor arrived life was found to be extinct. FOR PURE PORK SAUSAGE, try ASHLEY'S.—Advt. Assessment Committee. A meetiAg of the Warrington Union Assessment Committee was held at the Workhouse yesterday morning, Mr. A. J. Jolley presiding. There were also present: -Messrs. J. Billing°, W. Gill, S. Davies, G. Mather, W. Houghton, A.Brooks, J. C. Sutton (clerk), R. R. Meacock (valuer), and W. H. Woodcock (assistant overseer for Warrington).—The valuer reported that the following properties had been surveyed and valued according to the committee's instructions :—Township of Warrington. Monks, Hall and Co., • Ltd., additions to steel works, gross £552, ratable £392. Township of Sankey. Mersey White Lead'Works, additions comprising new stocks and gangways, paint store &amp;c., gross £2ll, ratable £160; Messrs. W. D. Houghton and Co., additions to Sankey Wire Mills, comprising new rope shed, stores &amp;C., gross £9O, ratable £76. With regard to the Cuerdley shooting, he had made inquiries locally and the information obtained tended to confirm the figures suggested last May, gross £2O, ratable £l9.—The report was adopted. DOULTON TOILET WARE and Dinner Sets. See Windows.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, • Warrington.—Advt., P.S.A. There was a fair attendance at Wycliffe P.S.A.. meeting on Sunday, when Councillor Hawthorn pre- sided, and referred to the annual meeting of the National Federation held in Liverpool last week, and the address given by the Rev. F. B. Meyer, the presid- ent. Mr. Hawthorn commented upon some of the principal points in the address, showing that good must come from within. All the improvements around them were good in their way, but when the inner life was made clear and beautiful the outer life became purer and more beautiful. Mr. George Fairbrother gave an address upon "Ideals" There were those whose lives were beautiful, but just a little marred with human defects, but in one Jesus Christ they found a complete ideal. Mr. James Mills sang "All ye that pass by" and Mr. W. H. Payton presided at the organ. The Secretary welcomed seven new mem- bers, making a total of 411. " HERALD " KITCHEN RANGES and " Peveril Fire Grates are unsurpassed. Inspection invited." —J. S. GARLAND, 51, 55, Bridge-street, Warrington. , —Advt.</p>
        <p>0 Mr. Burns has I believe, (writes the London correspondent of the " Liverpood Post and Mercury " ) 'come back from the Continent with strong opinions as to the superiority of the equipment of the British soldier over his French and German rivals, with both Of whom he has been recently on the march. He is Particularly emphatic on the better quality of the foot- gear and leg-gear of the British soldier. He has .come to the opinion that our putties are simply ideal, and generally regards the whole equipment of Tommy Atkins" as superior to that of the French or German soldier. He refuses to discuss the relative physique of the soldiers of the three countries, but he says that while the French and German soldiers are 'fine fellows, he still believes in" Tommy Atkins." O One never knows the limits of municipal enterprise. The Salford Town Council has decided upon the form- ation of a municipal choral society and a professional 'conductor has been appointed. The object of the society is the study of classical musical works, oratorios, operas, and so on. There are no entrance fees nor subscriptions; the Corporation provide room, light, piano or organ, so that the only expense left is the providing of music. The Corporation meet the ,Society even in this. They undertake to supply all slip" music —that is, such things as glees, madrigals, and so on—and the only thing that members will be asked to get for themselves will be a copy of whatever standard work the Society may be tackling. Salford is the first Northern town to adopt the experiment— which has much to recommend it —and developments will be awaited with interest. A period of great political activity is in store for Warrington, and the Liberals are bnsy making arrangements for meetings to be addressed by prominent members of the party. The Women's Liberal Association and the Progressive Labour League will hold a joint tea and public meeting in lhe Parr Hall on Monday October 28th. Mr. A. H Crosfield 1;5 M.P. will preside and will be accompanied ' Miss Cros'field. The speakers will include Mr. *dames Tomkinson, M.P., for Crewe. Other members of Parliament who it is hoped will visit Warrington during the winter are Mr. Harvey M.P., Mr. Stanley 11. P., and Mr. Fenwick M.P., who are all well-known representatives of Labour and have rendered yeoman service in the progressive cause. It is anticipated that one meeting will be addressed by a member of the Cabinet.</p>
        <p>Warrington Philomathic Society. The first meeting of the new session of the above society was held on Wednesday, the President (Mr. E. G. Burgess) in the chair. The members turned up in good numbers to hear the President's opening address upon the "Personality of Parnell." The President, in preface, appealed to the members of the society to do all in their plwer to make it a success for they possessed the ability to do so. They were trying to succeed in Warrington where men of greater age and experience had previously failed. Be believed that they were being. watche4 by men inter- ested in such societies ; it was, therefore, of paramount importance that no stone should be left unturned in its interests. Every member possessed an influence which he could uSe for the benefit of the Society. Turning to the subject of his address, the President said that Parnell owed his power .to his personality rather than to any intellectual genius or moral piety. Even Gladstone had to bend to his iron will. His life was one long succession of triumphs and failures ; and although his career ended with failure, Parnell never lost heart. To the last he fought for the cause which, during the whole of his political career, he had espoused. No man had ever held like him the destinies of a country in the hollow of his hand,and no man would have cast them, as Parnell to all appear- ances did, so ruthlessly rside. He was to be pitied not blamed, and whatever view was taken of the diffi- cult problem of his life, no one need hesitate to reckon him amongst those who had earned the title "Great." —A vote of thanks to the President for his address was proposed by Mr. J. H. Mason, seconded by Mr. F. J. Dunne, and carried with enthusiasm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Music In the Park. The Salvation A.rmy Band will play in the Park at '3.15 on Sunday afternoon under the conductorship of Mr. James Lee.</p>
        <p>"Yeomen of the Guard." Reserved seats fur the performances of " The Yeomen of the Guard" which takes place in the Parr Hall during the week commencing October 14th, can now be booked at Mr. ft. H. Pearson's, Bridge-street. The souvenir programme, with photos of principals, is also on sale. WHITE TOASTING eI:IMF:Ad. per lb.at ASHLEY'S.—Advt. The Lawson Contract. The arbitration proceedings in connection with this much-discussed contract will commence at the Surveyors' Institute, London,on Monday, October 14th. before Mr. George Hill, the arbitrator appointed by the High Omit. The amount claimed by Mr. Lawson is £37,000. Mr. Ratcliffe, K. 0., will appear for the Claimant and Mr. A. Hudson will represent the Corporation.</p>
        <p>Fairfield Schools. In this month's "Parish Church Magazine" refer- ence is made to the question of the continuance of the Upper Girls' school at Fairfield, which is now occupy- ing the attention of the Education Committee, and is stated that" The managers are determined not to lose their school if they can help it." What they intend to do is to press for a good staff so that higher subjects may be taught. 'Temptation to Thieves." The'Mayor of Warrington, at the Police Court on Wednesday, issued a strong warning to shopkeepers about placing their goods outside the shop doors, and thus offering undue temptation to thieves. A boy named William Charles Condliffe (14), Dakins-yard, was sent to a reformatory ,school for five years for stealing a pair of boots from outside the shop of Messrs. Stead &amp; Simpson,Horsemarket-street.The Bench disallowed the expenses of witnesses connected with the firm, and said they wished they could confiscate the stolen property.</p>
        <p>School of Art.—Loan Exhibition. There is on exhibition at the School of Art a collec- tion of specimens of design in materials which have teen loaned from South Kensington, which are of interest and are well worth a visit. They have been chosen by the Head Master, Mr. C. Mawson, A.R.C.A., and he will be pleased to see visitors at the school and explain any of the exhibits. In addition to the designs there are a few works which have been done by students in various Schools of Art and have been Purchased by the Board of Education.</p>
        <p>• Mayor Entertains School Children. There was a merry party at the Town Hall last evening when the Mayor and Mayoress entertained the 1305% and girls who, on the votes of their fellow- scholars at the elementary schools, were the recipients of the medals and brooches presented by the Mayor and Mayoress on Empire Day. The guests numbered about 80, and included the children of members of the Council. Everything was done for their enjoyment, and a right happy time did they spend. The Town Hall presented a very pretty scene. The balcony and steps outside were decorated with flags and bunting, while the interior of the building. was bright with vane-coloured electric lights. The introduction of groups of plants heightened the effect. After being 'heartily welcomed by the Mayor and Mayoress, the Youthful guests sat aownto an excellent tea. The rest of the evening was spent' in music and dancing, and the children were cordial in their appreciation of the thoughtfulness which prompted their host and hostess to arrange such a pleasant function. ,e Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, for Coughs and Olds; never fails. 1/1i 2/9.—Advt. • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 5, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 5, 1907.</p>
        <p>Personal. The Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst) attended the reception given by the Mayor and Mayoress of Eccles on Tuesday evening, when about 400 guests were received. The Mayors and Mayoresses of Salford and Leigh were also present. Many of our readers will be pleased to bear that Quartermaster George Wallace, who was stationed at Orford Barracks some three years ago, has this week been gazetted captain. KEIL.BITTTER, Is. per Ib.—ASHLEY'S, Sankey st.—Adv Bankruptcy Court. At the Warrington Bankruptcy Court yesterday, before Mr. Registrar Ridgway, two examinations were down for hearing -the cases of Aaron Clarke, 239, Liverpool-road, Warrington (adjourned from the last Court) and Frederick James Whittle, Marquis of Granby Inn, Church-street.—On the application of Mr. Johnson, assistant official receiver, both casBs were adjourned until November Ist.</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosflelds' Speech at Belle Vae. There was a slight error in our report of the speech delivered at Belle Vue by Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. The Member was stated to have said : —He knew that even what they had done made many good but timid people say "0 you dreadful people. You are work- ing not for a fall. You are working for revolution." (Laughter.) They never made a bigger mistake-- The sentence quoted should have read "0, you dreadful people, you are working not for reform. You are working for revolution." TABLE, FLOOR AND HANGING LAMPS. New Designs now showing.—J, S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge street, Warrington.—Advt. Death of Mrs. T. Jones. Many of our readers will deeply regret to hear of the death of Mrs. Jones (widow of the late Mr. Thomas Jones, who for many years carried on business as an ironfounder under the title of " W. Jones and Sons ") who passed away at her residence, Bewsey-street, on Sunday afternoon, after a long illness borne with great fortitude. Mrs. Jones, who was a native of Warrington, was a member of a very old Warrington family, her grandfather, Mr. John Buckley, carrying on the business of a coachbuilder so far back as the begioning-' of • last century, Her husband was for about twenty years a member of the Warrington' Town Council. She leaves a large family to mourn her loss. The funeral took place at the Cemetery on Wednesday afternoon. The Rev. J. Manning, of Preston, and until recently -minister at Bold-street Wesleyan Church with which the deceased was long identified, conducted the service and was assisted by the Rev. Thomas Miller. The chief mourners were : Messrs. William, Arthur, Frank, Percy, Ernest, I Harry and Charles Jones (sons). John Fairhurst, i George Weaver, Frank Inglis, of Heaton Chapel, and William Elliott, of Pendleton (sons-in-law), Mrs. Saunders, Mrs. Jones, Miss Jones (sisters-in-law), and Miss Lucy Jones, of Southport, (niece). Amongst others present were :—Messrs. L. Greening, F. R. Roberts, Joseph Craik (Blackpool), and Joseph Halsall (Widnes). Many beautiful wreaths were sent by relatives and friends. PUBLICS BENEFIT, UNDER WIN CLOCK FOR BEST ROOTS p LANCASHIRE.—Advt. •</p>
        <p>Mr. John White Honoured. On Tuesday evening Mr. and Mrs. John White were the recipients of handsome presents from the Conservatives of Warrington in recognition of their beg services to the party. The presentation took place at the Conservative Club in the presence of a large gathering. A magnificent silver rose bowl and a splendidly fitted travelling bag were the gifts to Mr. White. The rose bowl bore the following inscription: "Presented to John White, Esq., 1907, by the members of the Warrington Conservative Association on his retirement from the position of chairman, in recognition of the valuable services rendered by him to the party during the past forty years." The pre- sentation to Mrs. White took the form of a diamond brooch. Sir Gilbert Greenall presided, and was supported by Lady Greenall, Mr. and Mrs. John White, Mr. and Mrs. Henry Greenall, Mr. W. P. Rylands and Mr. and Mrs. Robert Pierpoint. Mr. Robert Pier point made the presentation, and paid a high personal tribute to Mr. White's energy and capacity, (Applause).—Lady Greenall handed the brooch to Mrs. White who expressed her hearty thanks.—Mr. White, in the course of a long speech, said his memory in politics went back to 1847—sixty years ago this July. (Applause.) He remembered the revered late Sir Gilbert Greenall then defeating Mr. Allcard by 327 votes to 297. He had many reasons for giving up the chairmanship of the association. He was not down- hearted because they were beaten at the last election. The chief reason was that he believed it preferable that when a change of that kind took_place it should occur when things were being re-organized, and a, new chairman should have ample opportunity of getting acquainted with the work and the workers before a general election.—An excellent concert was given, the arrangements for which had been made by Mr. C. P. Gough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>UNIVERSITY EXTENSION LECTURES.</head>
        <p>UNIVERSITY EXTENSION LECTURES.</p>
        <p>" Plant Life."</p>
        <p>The first of a course of six lectures on "Plant Life," arranged by the Warrington Education Committee in conjunction with the Manchester University, was delivered in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Wednesday evening by Mr. F. E. Weiss, D.Sc., pro- fessor of botany in the University of Manchester. There was a large attendance. Canon Morley Stevenson, who presided, said many of thoae present probably knew Professor Weiss person- ally, and more of them,perhaps,by reputation,and they knew he was going to deal with a subject of which he was a past master. The committee had this year made a change in the subject of the course. They bad for a time deserted the field of literature for the field of science. It was well, no doubt, that they should have changes and not always keep in the same groove, and he had very great hopes that the subject would not only prove intensely interesting to many of them who were lovers of nature—and let him say thank- fully that the number of those who were lovers of nature was certainly on the increase—but also that it would prove of great use to a large number of their teachers who had for some years been trying to give lessons in nature study; and doing it with very great effect in the schools. (Apnlallse.) Professior Weiss said he felt that what Canon Stevenson bad said as to his being a past master of the subject was a little superlative. He would rather regard himself as a student of botany, as he hoped to regard his audience also, and speak to them as a student to students, as a lover of plants to lovers of plants. The work which was being done in Manchester —in which they had enlisted the co-operation of Warrington —in regard to spreading a love of plants among the wnrkingclass population was being carried forward with great effect. Anyone who wished to deal with the question of raising plants and growing them under good conditions must obviously require and. desire a knowledge of plant life, that was to say; of the living proceseea which could be seen by any observer taking place in plants, and of the conditions which were favourable or might be nn favourable to the.life of plants. To study plant life properly they must observe the living plants. That, he was glad to say, had been recognized by educational authorities, and the cultivetion of plants, the germination of seeds, and experiments with living plants were one of the features which he thought all teachers vere endeavour- ing to bring home to the minds of their classes. The lecturer then dealt with the various parts of the plant and their response to the force of gravity; the roots and their functions ; the leaf and its varions parts ' • the relationship of leaves to light; the struggle for light among plants: and so on. He pointed out how plants rave off oxygen, and it was therefore important that we should have plants growing near us. That was why they were endeavouring to create more healthy sum-minding,* by growing more plants and supplying the vegetat;on which bad been so largely destroyed in mannfactnring towns. It was necessary to purify the atmosphere and get rid of the smoke because only in light places could plants assimilate and readily grew. The lectures will be continued every alternate Wednesday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>SIGNALMAN DIES ON DUTY.</head>
        <p>SIGNALMAN DIES ON DUTY.</p>
        <p>A singalman named William Marshall, who worked on the Furness Railway at Ulverston, was found dead at his post on Tuesday morning by the guard of a goods train. The driver of the train was unable to get his signal to proceed, and after whistling several times called the guard's attention to the circumstance that the deceased bad not been in good health of late.</p>
        <p>For Children's Hacking Cough at night, Woods' Great Peppermint Cure. 1/li, 2/9.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. 'The Bondman."</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. 'The Bondman."</p>
        <p>Hall Caine's famous play "'the Bondman has attracted huge audiences to the Royal Court Theatre this week, the occasion of its first visit to Warrington. Much interest was taken in the play, especially as the chief part, Jason, was in the hands of Mr. Frank Cooper who played with Mrs. Pat Campbell in Mr. Arthur Collins' production of the piece at Drury Lane. The story of the play is too well-known to need more than outlining here. Suffice it to say that Jason and Michael Sunlocks, half-brothers who are unknown to each other, fall in love with Greeba,the daughter of a Manx farmer. Michael wins her love with the opportunities afforded by constant association in the same house and Jason only appears on the scene as an outcast and deserter. He has travelled all the way from Sicily to be avenged on the son of the father who betrayed his mother, and arrives to find that this son, Michael, has set out by , the same ship to seek him with the most benevo- lent and brotherly intentions. The plot, of course, thickens after this, as Jason remains on the farm and falls in love with Greeba. Then the scene changes to Sicily where Michael has performed wonders and gained the Presidency of the Island. Greeba goes out and marries him, Jason following with re-awakened bitter- ness and fresh vows of vengeance. That vengeance is finally turned to love, both brothers being sent to the sulphur-mines and there becoming comrades in ignor- ance of their relationship. When the secret is dis- covered lason has a moment's mental agony, but the great good nature of the man triumphs and he saves Michael's life. In the final scene Jason sacrifices freedom, love, and life for the half-brother he had once sworn to kill. We need hardly- say that Mr. Frank Cooper is superb in the fine role of Jason, and both looks and acts as if to the Sicilian born. Jason is the mainstay of the play and without his dominating influ- ence it would not rise far above the realms of mediocrity. Mr. Henry Doughty made a capital Michael Sunlocks with the exception that his locks were raven black. His acting was manly and vigorous, or restrained, as occa- sion demanded. Warm praise is also due to Mr. Edwin Palmer, who made a capital old salt in the role of "grandfather." Mrs. Edwin Palmer was no less successful as the matronly and essentially practical Mrs. Fairbrother. Her husband, a man who has "swallowed the New Testament at a gulp" is all the more a good-hearted sincere gentleman and the part is well-played by Mr. Adam Alexander. Miss Dorothy Garth, as Christian Ann, the merry maid-servant, also achieves a distinct success, but we most confess to some disappointment with Miss Ida Philips, as the heroine, Greeba. She makes the part too melancholy and generally spends a more miserable time than we should imagine the author intended. The cast is a very heavy one and revgals the resources of a capable company. A word should be said of the mount- ing of the play, which is excellent. The dramatic, scene in the sulphur mines is exceedingly well-done,and lacks nothing in realism or horror. Since the company arrived in Warrington, one mem- ber has had the misfortune to meet with an accident. Mr. Charles Medwin, who was billed to fill the role of Tomasso Testa, was unable to do so owing to being knocked down and run over by a motor-car at Market Gate on Monday. He sustained such injuries to his foot that necessitated his removal to the Infirmary where he is now an inpatient.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Next Week's Programme. "THE NIGHT OF THE PARTY."</head>
        <p>Next Week's Programme. "THE NIGHT OF THE PARTY."</p>
        <p>We have to congratulate the Royal Court management upon engaging for next week Mr. Weedon Grossmith's celebrated company with "The Night of the Party," which ran in London for over 1,000 nights. The com- pany will include that talented actress Miss Mab Paul, who will be remembered as leading lady with the late Sir Henry Irving. The</p>
