      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>MISS MAB PAUL,</head>
        <p>MISS MAB PAUL,</p>
        <p>_ "Daily Telegraph "said of her:?—" Sir Henry-Irving has secured the scrvices of Miss Mab Paul for the part of 'Beatrice' in Dante,' for England and America. Miss Paul is a fine actress and can boast a magnificent stage presence, either of which would fully justify the many times her portrait has been hung in the Royal Academy." It is to be hoped there will be bumping houses again next week and thus encourage good companies of this class to visit Warrington. This is the same company that has visited Liverpool, Manchester, Birmingham, and all the principal cities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>INFIRMARY EXTENSION.</head>
        <p>THE INFIRMARY EXTENSION.</p>
        <p>HOW THE WORK IS PROGRESSING</p>
        <p>Warrington will soon bo able to pride itself upon the possession of one of the most up-to-date and splendidly equipped Infirmaries in the country. The extension which the ever-increasing needs of the town made imperative, is rapidly nearing completion and it is expected that the new block of buildings, com- prising a pavilion and nurses' home, will be ready for opening in November. An ""Examiner" representa- tive visited the building the other day and was able to form a gold impression of the improved means of tending the sick and injured which will be placed at the disposal of the community when the work is finished. One of the great advantages of the scheme is ' that nurses and patients will be housed under separate roofs,for it is eminently desirable that the nurses should have a change of air and surroundings when off duty. The nurses' home will ac3ommodate twenty-five nurses and it is erected on the most modern lines. On the ground Elor are the sitting rooms, a large dining room, and the kitchen. The second and third storeys are devoted to the nurses' bedrooms, each nurse having a separate bedroom. There are two bathrooms on each fllor and 'the lavatory accommodation is exceptionally good. The servants' quarters occupy, the fourth storey. The building is heated through- out by hot water. Passing on to the new pavilion,we find it consists of three wards, only two of which will, however, be utilized for the time being. It was considered wise, however, to build the three, wards and thus avoid the heavier expense of another exten- sion at some future date. , Each ward will accom- modate 22 beds and the air space allotted to each bed is considerably above the average,being 1,456 cubic feet. The rooms are heated by hot water by means of radiators, and there are also two stoves in each ward. The latest idea of ventilation has been adopted, all the inlets being under the beds. The result is that the patient gets the purest. air, and there is no risk of draughts. At the head of each bed is an electric lamp. Adjoining the wards are the lavatories and bathrooms, these lving shut off from the ' Infirmary and ventilated as separate buildings. The walls are so 'designed that it is impossible for dust to accumulate in the wards. The finors are fire- proof and a fireproof staircase leads from one ward to the other. On each floor there is a verandah, where convalescents will be able to enjoy the fresh air, and as the aspect is southerly there will be more or less sunshine all the day. Altogether the ,buildings are admirably adapted .for the purpose for which they are intended and reflect credit upon the architects (Messrs. W. and S. Owen) and the builder (Mr. C. W. Daven- port). It is gratifying to note that the extension fond now amounts to about £14,000, the bazaar at. Hill ICliffs having realized over £2,200.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREAM</p>
        <p>CRACK ERS.</p>
        <p>CRISP /sr) CREAMY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
