eputy clerk) T. Cooke. J. Pearson, and W. R. D. Prez ,relieving officers) ; and J. Cunnala (master). vac C [NATION FEES. The Clerk produced a letter signed by Dr. MacDougall, Dr. Ourruthers (Halton), and Dr. Burton (Frodsham) as follows :—Yonr letter addressed to us individually on September 18th, we prefer to answer jointly as heretofore. We cannot but express our deep regret that the Guardians' have not thought tit to meet ns re fees as we thought we had made out a fair claim for generous treatment. We do not propose to carry the matter farther for although we consider the fees low and inadequate we have decided to accept the terms offered by the Guardians. Dr. Owen, of Stretton, wrote accepting the revised fees as laid down by the Board. FINANCE. Mr. Woodward read a report of the Finance Com- mittee, which showed that accounts were passed amounting to £453 ss. Sd., leaving a balance in hand of £5,56525. 7d.</p>
        <p>REIRAL DISTitICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting' of this council was held at Dutton Work- house on Tuesday, Mr. G. Pilling presidine. There were also present:—Messrs. J.H. Hallows. T. Wright, S. Woodward, G. Egerton-Warburton, Admiral Jones- Parr; A. Norman, J. J. Jones. J. Spruce, J. IT. Davies, E. Britch, A. Broome, P. Booth, T. Wood P. Collins J. Haining., G. F. Ashton (clerk), J. Di ggle and W. Swinton (surveyors), and J. Farrington (nuisance inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RUNCORN WOHAN SENTENCED.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN WOHAN SENTENCED.</p>
        <p>NOTIFICATION_ OF BIRTHS._</p>
        <p>The Clerk said the Local Government Board had sent notice of passing of the following Acts: The Notifica- tion of Births Act 1907 and the Expiring Laws Continuation Act, 1907. The object of the first Act which would only be in operation when it was adopted by the local authority wittt the consent of the Board or was declared to be in form bY' the Board, was to provide a speedy means whereby information of the birth of a child might be given to the Medical Officer of Health that if necessary advice might be given by the Medical Officer of Health mother in regard to the rearing of the child.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED NUISANCH AT DARF.SBUR V.</p>
        <p>The Clerk stated that Mr. Ernest Ridgway had for- warded a cony of an analrsis he had had taken of the water in the brook which ran through Mr. Ridgway's grounds at Daresbury Lodge. At two points there was much contamination and it required immediate attention. He should be glad to hear that the Council had instructed the nuisance to be remedied.</p>
        <p>TFIE WESTON ROADMEN. It was reported that at a meeting of the Weston Parochial Committee it was resolved to recommend the District Council that the Weston roadmen should be kept permanently employed in the parish and not be taken to assist in other parishes with the road roller. It was decided that the clerk lav before the Council at the next meeting the information as to the lighting of the street lamps obtained by the committee when the question was last before them.</p>
        <p>NORLEV WATER. The Clerk produced tenders in reference to the Norley Water Contract which were 83 follows :—Mr. .1. Barrow (Frodsham), Zl 79. 6d. ; W. Blair 329. Gd. ;F. W. Spencer, one guinea, and W. P.Neetor, of Liverpool,£l. The last-named contract was accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>STEPPING DOWNW kiIDS.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens, on Saturday, John Cunningham, a Man- chester painter, was sent to gaol for three months with hard labour for stealing a step-ladder on November 13th last year. On that date Tie borrowed a huller, pretend- ing that he had SOMA work to do in Water-street, and he immediately stole it and left the town. He had written a statement to the magistrates, in which he deeply regretted his pist career, and pleaded for leniency, saying that he committed the offence because he was starving. DRUNKEN SCENES AT A WAKE.</p>
        <p>Micheal Nree was charged at St. Helens, on Saturday with breach of the peace. Police-constable Smith said he was called to the prison's house, 13, Liverpool- street, and found several people drunk, and a child that had died the previous dly on a table in the room. There was a great disturbance, and he had to lock the man up. He reported his wife for drunkenness. Mrs. Mee appeared in court, and was also charged. The police gave her a very bad cha.racter,and said the trouble really arose from a wake that was being held on the child. Mee was ordered to fled sureties or go to prison for fourteen days, and Mrs. Mee was fined 14s, or fourteen days. Time was allowed to raise the money and bury the child.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS GLASSWORKS STOPPAGE. COMPENSATION FOR APPRENTICES.</p>
        <p>Six cases arising out of the stoppage of the St. Helens Junction Glassworks, owned by Messrs. Turner and Co., Limited, early in July, came before the St. Helens magistrates on Monday. Mr. P. C. Kelly ssid that John Hughes and Charles Murray, two apprentices, had summoned the limited company under the indentures entered into after the business had been turned over from Messrs. J. B. Turner and R. N. Turner to the company. He had had a long correspond- ence with the solicitors representing the firm, but they desired to have those two cases adjourned until Friday.—The bench agreed.—Mr. Kelly said that four apprentices whose indentures were made with the Brothers Turner before the limited company was formed were proceeding. Samuel Whitdeld, Frederick Taylor, Herbert Moss, and James Lomax asked for £lO damages under the Employers and Work- men Act. Whitfield said he was apprenticed to be a leadlight maker, but had now taken up gardening. He asked for the indentures to be cancelled. Mr. Kelly said the trouble had been caused by the stoppage of the works and the reconstruction of the company. He understood there was no ides of defending these cases. The bench awarded £5 damages and advocates's fee, and cancelled the indentures. Frederick Taylor was appren- ticed as a beveller, and had already lost .£9 2s. in wages. Awarded £lO and advocate's fee. Herbert Moss said he had succeede'cl in getting back to his trade in another works. Awarded £5 and costs. James Lomax was apprenticed to be a glass beveller. Awarded £lO and 0 Bth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>This beautiful new entertainment house has attracted crowded audiences during the week, and all have been delighted with the bright and tuneful programme pre- sented. The leading feature is again provided by John Tiller's troupe of forty performers, who appear in the musical comedy extravaganza" A Trip to Paris." This is one of the finest productions ever placed on the variety stage, and there is laughter loud and long at the escapades of Eli Heggs from Oldham and Hiram P. Gibson from Chicago while on a visit to the gay city. Some exceedingly amusing complications ensue, and the play goes with a capital swing from beginning to end. Bob Selvidge as the American and Teddy Gibbs as Eleggs take the chief honours, the audiences being convulsed by the doings of these clever comedians. The company, all round, is an excellent one, and the scenery is very effective. The rest of the programme is highly entertaining, the best turn being that of Lawson and Namon who go through a remarkable comedy, trick cycling, and ball-punching act. Moon and Morris are capital comedians and simultaneous dancers, and McDonald and Huntington give an original American entertainment. Some fine pictures are exhibited by means of the Raymond Bio-Tableau x.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>'NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>'NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>There will boa complete change of programme next week and some exceptionally attractive tnrns are announced. Madge Grey, dialect comedienne, heads the bill and another novel item will be provided by Shirley Higgins and Co., vocalists, in "Farmyard pastimes." The Delomes, duettists and champion dancers of the world, will appear in their speciality "The phantom clogs." The other artists who will contribute to a most interesting and varied programme are :—Sisters Godfrey, vocalists and acrobatic dancers; Wild, Willie and West, in their comedy acrobatic trampoline and trap scene "The bounding navvies " ; Prince Kokin, famous Japanese juggler; Albert Letine, characteristic impersonator ; and Rosalie Jacobi, soubrette.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>(Continued from Page 60 Services for _Sunday.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church: (Ha-rvest Festival). Morning 10.30, Rev. W. Mellor; evening 6.30, Rev. Thomas Miller. Golborne-street Baptist Church: (Harvest Festival). Morning 10.45 and evening 6.30, Rev. Robert E, Letheren. In the afternoon at 2.30 there will be a service of song entitled "Joyful Harvesters." Baptist Church Latchford; Morning 10.30 and evening 6.30, Rev. J. F. Matthews. Wycliffe, Church. Bowsey-street : Morning 10.30 and evening 6.30, Rev. J. Yonge. Evening subject: Christ's "Other Sheep." Cairo-street: (Harvest Festival). Morning 10.45, afternoon 2.15 and evening 6.30, Rev. W. E. Williams, B.A. Bewsey-road, Wesleyan Church: Morning 10.30, Rev. E. J. Gawne ; evening 6.20, Rev. W. Mellor. WARRLNGTON PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Services of thanksgiving for the ingathering of the harvest were commenced at this church on Thursday evening when there was a large- congregation. The church had been very beautifully decorated, the work being undertaken by the following :—Altar, Miss Taylor and Mr. Derby; pulpit, Mrs. Bottomley and Mtg. Fowler; font, Mrs. R. Lunt and Mrs. Hall; chancel rails, Miss Price and Miss Brookes: clargv and choir stalls, Mrs. Farrall, Mrs. Locker, Misses Willis, Lilian Cmik and Lydiard ; windows and pendants, Mrs. J. Hill and Miss G. Taylor. The service was conducted by the Rev. T. A. J. Ridpath, the lessons being read by the Rector and the Rev. G. E. Lejeune. The preacher was the Rev. Dr. Cogswell, Rector of Wallasey, who based an eloquent sermon on the words: "Except a corn of wheat fall into the ground and die, it abideth alone; but if it die it bringeth forth much fruit."( John xii., 24). The choir are to be complimented upon their tasteful rendering of the musical portions of the service. The anthem was" Lord of the harvest" (Barnby) and the canticles were sung to Bunnett's well-known settings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>ST. PAUL'S CHURCFT.</head>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHURCFT.</p>
        <p>The services will he continued on Sunday when the preachers will he :—Morning Rev. G. Peters, Vicar of St. Thomas', Wavertree ; afternoon at 2.30 children's service addressed by the Vicar; 3.0, united Bible Clasaes Services, preacher Rev. E. R. Tarbuck ; even- ing, Rev. E. R. Tarbnck.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>HOLY TRINITY CHURCH.</head>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services commenced op Thursday evening when the Rev. W. H. Money, M.A., vicar of Northwich, preached to a large congregation. To-morrow (Sunday) the preachers will he :—Morning, Rev. T. T. Lee Jones, M.A., vicar of Dareshurv' • afternoon. the Vicar; evening, Rev. F. \V. E. Chadwick, 8.A., of Winwick.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>ST. JAMES', LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>ST. JAMES', LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. E. Thorp, L.Th., occupied the pulpit at this church on Thursday, when the harvest thanksgiving services were commenced. There was a very good con- gregation. The preacher said certain farmers had told him that the month of September was all that could he desired, and he was pleased to say they had had a very good harvest indeed, so that there was every need to be thankful.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Davies presided at the organ and great praise is due to him for the way in whieh he lei the choir in the rendering of the anthem " He giveth all things " (Clare). The Magnificat and Nun o Dimmitis were sung to Bunnett's setting in F. BEWSEY-ROAD WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>There were large congregations at the harvest thanks giving services held at this church on Sunday. In the morning the preacher was the Rev. William Mellor,who based his discourse on the text " Give us this day our daily bread," and showed how dependent we were upon the fruits of the earth. In the afternoon the choir gave a highly creditable rendering of ft cantata entitled "Song of thanksgiving." Mr. W. Hipkiss conducting. The cantata included the following items which were very effectively sung:—duet (soprano and tenor) "The eyes of all wait upon Thee," Miss Leicester. Mr. Wood; soorano solo, "When o'er the trees of Eden " Miss S. Silcock ; tenor solo, "Thus saith the Lord "Mr. Wood ; trio, "While the earth remaineth "Misses F. Leicester and L. Evans, Mr. C. Sinclair ; contralto solo. " Consider the lilies " Miss M. Pitt ; soprano solo." 0 lovely flowers.blest messenger divine" Miss M. Hipkiss; duet (soprano and tenor) "The blushing fruit:: appear at His command "Miss Hipkiss Mr. Hannan; bass solo, Mr. G Gough : tenor solo, "0 Thou, whose constant mercies" Mr. Hannan. Mr. W. Pearce was at the organ. Mr. Isaac Hayward presided, and gave an interesting address, showing that 1 it was the duty of all to do good in the circle in which they moved, and thus to make the world better. The preacher in the evening was the Rev. E. J. Gawne, who took for his text the words "They that sow in tears shall reap in joy." In the evening the music included the beautiful chorus "0 come, let us sing." The collections at the morning and 'evening services were in aid of the society funds, and were an increase on those realized last year. The offertory in the afternoon will be devoted to the trust fund. The church had been very artistically deccrated with harvest products, the work reflecting credit upon the ladies of the con- gregation. On Mondey evening there was a successful sociaban admirable musical programmebeingsubmitted</p>
        <p>THE END OF UNCLE TOM'S CABIN. Uncle Tom's Cabin, the well-known hotel at Black- pool, closed its door at eleven o'clock on Thursday night for ever. The licence was transferred by the Kirkham magisirates to the new Uncle Tom's cabin, which has been erected some distance away from the old building.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>ST. HELENS MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>I Mr. Morris Darby, who is well-known In Liverpool where he was formerly a Protestant lecturer, disclaims any intention of playing the part of Mr. Hunnable in. the St. Helens municipal elections. Mr. Darby had carried on business in St. Helens for some years past, and he is coming forward as an independent candidate for South Windle Ward. He states that he is connected with no party, bat means to fight, and that he will have the support of most of the Catholic voters in the ward ST. HELENS UNITARIAN CHANGES.</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. P. Farley, 8.A., who for fonr years has been in charge of the new Unitarian church in Corpora- tion-street, St. Helens, on Monday night said farewell to the congregation at a social presided over by the Rev. J. Collins Odgers (president of the Liverpool and District Unitarian Association). Testimony to the ability and the whole-hearted enthusiasm shown by Mr. and Mrs. Farley was borne by the speeches of Messrs. T. Trantes, D. Finney, and J. Hewitt, the Rev. H. W. Hawkes, and the Rev. Morley iills, of Bootle. Mr. Farley leaves St. Helens for work in the slums of North London, and he is succeeded at St. Helens by the Rev. J. Bellamy Higham, 8.A., of Park-lane, Ashton, who was introduced to the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>BURTONWOOD VICAR AND BOOKMAKER. The Vicar of Buttonwood (Rev. A. M. Mitchell M.A.) has resigned his seat on the Lancashire Education Sub-Committee, which meets at Earlestown. In a _ letter to the chairman he says :—" For some time past I have debated with myself the consistency of one holding my views remaining a member of an Education Committee to which certain gentlemen have gained admission whose business or trade is calculated to demoralise and pauperise the people. I have looked at this matter from many points of view, and can see no other way to consistent conduct and a restful mind. I must be excused if I decline to sit on the same Com- mittee of Education with a notorious bookmaker, who has been fined, I believe at least a dozen times in the police court. That such a state of things sl•ould be possible is, to me, a grievous slander, which ought never to have been created, and which cannot be too speedily terminated. My hope is that this action of mine, which involves some self-sacrifice and consider- able pain, may be the means of directing attention to a. state of things which is so great a dishonour to citizen- ship, arid so serious a hindrance to education and social righteousness. Strong remedies are sometimes necessary for bad diseases." CONSERVATIVE CLUB "SMOKER." A smoking concert and presentation of the Legh Challenge Cup, &amp;c., took place on Saturday night at the Earlestown Conservative Club. Prior to the concert a hot-pot supper was served to which a good muster of members sat down, the meal being admirably served by the steward and stewardess, Mr. and Mrs. Sutton. The chair was taken by Mr. Borron (president of the Club), and an excellent musical programme was gone through. During an interval the cup was presented by the Chairman to the winners, the Earlestown Conservative Club Billiard Team. Mr. Siddeley as captain, suitably responded. In conjunction with the billiard competition whist matches were also played, and the Earlestown Club players were successful. In lieu of medals a case of carvers was presented to each player. The recipients were—Billiard team : Messrs. TT Siddeley (captain),J. Hill, E. Weekes, F. Walker, C. Wilson, B. Weekes W. Cheeeeborough, A. qballcross, E. Davies' R. H. Carson, J. Tunstall, G. Sutten. Whist team: Messrs. Joseph Smith (captain), T. Bowes, John Smith, R. Roberts, 'John Shallcross, Joseph Shallcross, J. Gee, J. A. Galloway, G. Owen, T. Fairclough, J. Clarke and W. Blackwell. After the presentation an address was given by Mr. G. F'. Clarke on the Kirkdale election, in which he took a prominent part. Mr. Edwin Leyland accom- panied the singers. SHOP, ASSISTANTS' GRIEVANCES. Mr. J. A. Seddon, I.P. was the principal speaker at. Birmingham on Sunday night at a meeting of shop assistants, at which a resolution was passed condemning the living-in system, radius" agreements, and over- time, and calling on the Government to remedy the evils. The Bishop of Birmingham wrote that the living-in system tended to more evil and discomfort than it remedied. He was strongly in favour of the organisation of all industrial interests, and of collective bargaining between them, with the further prospect of an ultimate court of appeal, which should in some way represent society as a whole. On Monday afternoon, at Toynbee Flail, Whitechapel, a well-attended Conference of employers and shop assistants' representatives was held for the purpose of considering the question of the system.—Lord Hugh Cecil, who presided, said the Conference had been called for the purpose of pro- curing an interchange of ideas connected with the sub- ject of living-in. It would not be in his power, owing to his lack of knowledge, to afford them much guidance, hut he trusted that their discussion would be fraught with good results.—Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P. said his union was pledged to the total abolition of the system. They held : (I) That it was deleterious to health. (2) That it prevented the growth of individuality and self-reliance. (3) That the celibate conditions imposed conduced to a low state of morality. (4) nit the system was economically unsound. (5) That it excluded the shopworkers from the social cud civil life of the community. (6) That modern commercialism had outgrown the system, and it was not necessary for the proper conduct of husinesa. . . .</p>
        <p>_ Those whom he represented asked that living-in should not he made a condition ef employment, and that employers should at once take steps to abolish the system. The Truck Inquiry Committee were preparing recommendations on the subject, and the union had asked that the Truck Acts should be extended to shop workers, viz., that their wages should be paid in full in the current coin of the realm,and the board and lodging, if provided by the employer, should not be a condition of employment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>CRYSTAL PALACE BAND CONTEST. Wingatas Again Successful. CROSFIELD'S WIN FIFrH PRIZE.</head>
        <p>CRYSTAL PALACE BAND CONTEST. Wingatas Again Successful. CROSFIELD'S WIN FIFrH PRIZE.</p>
        <p>The eighth annual championship band festival was held at the Crystal Palace on Saturday, and proved an enormous attraction and success. Nothing else short of a Cup-tie could bring the people of the North to London in such crowds. They travelled through Friday night in crowded trains, and by One o'clock on Saturday morning handsnien in new uniforms were doing a little quiet sight-seeing amidst the Covent Garden market vans and the newspaper carts. Visitors poured into the Palace all day on Saturday, till there were some 60,000 present above the number which is expected on an average August Bank Holiday. Mr. J. Henry lies, who lhas been intimately associated with the noteworthy tours of the Besses-ti-th'-Barn band, was in charge of the contest. Be expressed his opinion during the day that Saturday's was the most successful of all the eight contests which have now been held. The talent dis- played was undoubtedly above the average The were nine events, chief among which, of course, was the thousand-guinea trophy, won last year by Win- gates Temperance Band, of Lancashire. This fine body of perform.rs was successful for a second time on Saturday. Their victory carries with it the champion- ship of Great Britain and the colonies, the National Challenge Trophy, value 1,000 guineas, an illuminated certificate and £4O in cash, and a bronze medal to each member of the band. The number of bands competing in the different events was 150. Only two of the com- petitions were held actually within the buildings, one of these being ,for the thousand-guinea trophy. Early in the morning there was a rehearsal for the massed band concert in the evening in the garden hall. Nine bands took part in this fine performance. They were Wingates Temperance, Goodshaw, King's Cross, Cros field's, Batley Old Band, Pemberton Old Band, Shaw- Wvke, and Rusbden Temperance. The competition for the thousand-guinea cup was played throughout the day. The test music selected for performance by the 17 competitors was a specially arranged selection of" Gems of Schumann,"for which Mr. William Short, conductor to tht London County Council, was responsible. There was plenty of specula- tion as to the result of the great competion, but Win- gates Temperance were undoubtedly first fay onrities. Mr. Short,Lieutenant Charles Godfrey,and Mr. Richard Stead, judging the performances from morning 'till night, were hidden in a gallery from which they were unable to identify the hands as they came upon the platform below. The enthusiasm of the great audiences who listened to the various performances, many follow_ ing the music with score in hand, was something to give wonder to those who believe that the English are not musical people. The trophy result was kept to the las The following were the results : Grand Championship Trophy, valued 1,000 guinea s With other prize° mentioned above-1, Wingates Temperance Band (holders) ; 2, Goodshaw ; 3, King's Cross ; 4, Rushden Temperance ; s,Crostield's Soapworks; 6, Gossage's Soaptyorks ; 7, Shaw. Junior Cup Section.-1, Bestwood Colliery :2, Peter- borough Excelsior; 3, Wrightington ; 4, Barnoldswick. Grand Shield Section.-1, South Apor •, 2, Raw- marsh; 3, Southwark Borough; 4, Middlesbrough Borough; 5, Raunds Temperance. Preliminary Shield Section.-1, Crown Silver ; 2, Rockingham Colliery; 3, Denaby Main Ambulance- 41 Barnsley Borough. Preliminary Cup Section.-1, Rothwell Rifles; 2, Rowntree's Cocoa Works (York) ; 3, Dinnington ; 4, Marri ners..</p>
        <p>-63 nsolation Cup Section.-1, Caerphilly ;2, Notting- ham Railway Associated City; 3, St. Pancras Prize; 4, Cud worth Old. R eed Band Section.-1, Ist V.B. Buffs; 2, Swindon Borough; 3, Dewsbury and District; 4, Reading Temperance. Concertina Band Section . —l, Oldham.; 2, Heywood; 3, Heckmondwike ; 4, Alexborough.</p>
        <p>For Bronchial Coughs take Woods' Great Peppermint ' Cure. 1/li, 219.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
