      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>SPIRITUALIST SOCIETY. A harvest festival was held in the Druid's Hall, on Sunday and Monday. The speaker was Mrs.E. Cropper, of Ashton-under-Lyne, who gave addresses on Sunday afernoon and evening suitable to the occasion, followed at each meeting with clairvoyant descriptions of departed friends. At the evening meeting the hall was crowded. The room had been decorated with ever- greens, flowers, corn, fruit, bread, and vegetables con- tribnted by many friends, a very good display being tnade. On Monday evening the meeting, at which Mrs. Cropper again officiated, giving medical and other psychometry and clairvoyance, was dosed at nine o'clock. and the fruit and vegetables sold for the benefit of the funds.</p>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, LYMNI. Many Warrington Baptists journeyed to this Church on Sunday when the Rev. H. Dunnico was the preacher.. 'The church had been tastefully decorated by Mesdames Barrett, J. H. Holt, Clayton, Simpson, and Porter, and flowers and fruit were sent by Mesdames Onston and Groves and Miss Grundr. There was a children's service in the afternoon. On 'Monday the usual social was held, when the fruit was sold, and the proceeds devoted to the poor fund.</p>
        <p>I afternoon the cantata " A Daughter of Moab" (Andrew) was admirably rendered by the choir,their singing being eulogized on every hand. Mr. J. Broadhurst presided. The principal soloists were: Miss A. Bridge (Ruth) Miss Linn Plinston (Naomi), Miss L. Snelling (Orpah), Mr. E. &amp;Pearce (Boaz); parts were also taken by Misses A. and B. Woodland, hfiss P. Cruikshank, and Mr. J. H. Corrin. At the evening service the anthem "They that sow in tears" (Rttson), was very creditably rendered by the choir as was also the solo S' Giver of Life " by Miss A. Woodland. On Monday evening a fruit banquet was held in the schoolroom presided over by Mr. W, Crampton, when songs "Come back to Erin" and : " Killarney " by Miss A. Woodland, and "They all love Jack "by Mr. A. Spruce; quartets, "Softly fall . the shades." "The sea bath its pearls " and " Moon- light and Music "by Misses A. and E. Woodland and (Messrs. Phillips and Spruce, were well rendered. The organist and accompanist at all the services was Mr. A. W. Clatworthy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>CHILDREN'S COUGHS Relieved at once by VENUS LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S COUGHS Relieved at once by VENUS LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Nothing could be safer, surer, and speedier than Veno's Lightning Cough Cure. Mr. Henry J. Rolls, of The Grove Lodge, Ma'pas, Newport, Mon., writes:—" I must tell you what a great blessing your Cough Cure has been to our little boy. He suffered for years with bronchitis and a chronic cough, and I tried endless cough syrups, embrocations and doctor's medicines in vain.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH. There was an exceedingly large congregation at this church on Wednesday evening. the occasion being the annual harvest festival. The Venerable Archdeacon Madden occupied the pulpit and preached from the text "And thou shalt rejoice in every good thing which the Lord thy God hath given unto thee and unto thine house, thou and the Levite and the stranger that is among yon." (Dent. xxvi. 11.) The choir gave the mnsical portion of the service in a highly creditable manner, the anthem being "I will sing of Thy mercies" (Barlc3r). The Magniflcat and Nun° Dirnmitis were sung to Stainer's settings in B flat. Mr. E. Wilkes presided at the organ. The church had been beautifully decorated by the members lady of the congregation. GOLBORNE-STREET BAPTIST CHURCH.. The harvest thanksgiving services in connection with this Church commenced on Thursday evening. There was a fair congregation, and the Rev. Charles Joseph (Cambridge) occupied the pulpit. He took for his text the words: "And they lifted up their voices and wept I CHURCH MISSION.—The annual harvest thanksgiving again and Orpah kissed her mother-in-law, but Ruth sermoes were preached by the Rev. %V. Pollock-Hill, of °lave unto her."—Ruth i., 14. He said that most people , Padgate, at the above place of worship on Wednesday rejected their mothers-in-law, but he had known , evening and attracted a large congregation. The room mothers-in-law to be better than mothers to some , was most tastefully decorated by th 3 Misses Tinsleys people. Continuing to expound the text, he referrEd to and other ladies of the congregation. During the the time when there was a famine in Moab. Naomi service part of a cantata entitled: "The rolling had been unfortunate in her doings, her sons had died, season's' was admirably rendered by the Penketh Choral so she returned to Bethlehem, and on arriving she bade Society. Miss B. Davies took the solo and their singing people not to call her Naomi, which means pleasant.,