      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 5, 1907.</p>
        <p>THE VOICE OF LABOUR. Edited by John Workman.</p>
        <p>Readers of this column are invited to assist in making it interesting and helpful to social workers by contributing themselves. Correspondents must write on one side of the paper only, and endeavour to make their letters and notes short and to the point. A nom-de-plume may be used, but the real name of the writer should be forwarded in confidence to the Editor of the column. 31.5.5. must be addressed to John Workman, " Examiner " Office, Warrington and should be sent in not later than Wednesday in each week.</p>
        <p>Robert Blatchford— the one and only—is coming to Warrington. He is to take the chair at the Socialist Demonstration in the Parr Hall, on Monday evening next.</p>
        <p>The Editor of "The Clarion" is sure of a great reception The principal speakers for Mr. Blatchford is of such a shy and retiring disposition that he will evade the Chairman's address if he is not carefully watched -are Mr. T. Summerbell, 11. P., and Mrs. Snowden, the wife of Philip Snowden, M.P. lam not going to press any of my readers to be present. All I can say is: if you want to learn something of Socialism, you'll be at the Parr Hall on Monday even- ing next. The tickets are 3d. and 6d. each. Ticket- holders are admitted at 6.45. Mr. Blatchford takes the Chair at 7.30. CHRISTIAN SOCIALISM.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles E. Parker is beginning to grumble because his friends have not backed him up in his argument. lam sorry. The title of the column is not agreeable to him either. If "Socialists have started and monopolised this column," as he so gracionsly points out, whose fault is it? Why'clo not the literary members of the Liberal Association, the Progressive Labour League. the Junior Liberals, the Conservative Association, the Conservative Labour Party,. the Junior Conserva- tives send in their contributions? Is it because they have nothing to say. Are they afraid of defending the present competitive system ? Or is it because Socialism is the only thing worth fighting for? Now to the real point at issue between us. Mr. Parker says: "Turning to the Editor I am still of the opinion that he is quite mistaken in his idea that the Christian Socialist favoured the Socialism or rather Communism which he advocates. The Editor appears to have written to a friend to find the origin of the quotation given from Maurice. This friend traces it to one of the tracts on Christian Socialism written in 1849, and adds another quotation. Before expressing a further opinion on these quotations I should like to study the context, because the life of Maurice written by his son,which I have just rem], appears entirely to contradict the interpretation which John Workman gives. If I find lam wrong in this, or any other thing, John Workman will have no reason to complain that my acknowledgment of any error is inadequate." _</p>
        <p>Mr. Parker will please no-te the following Particulars. As I write I have before me a tract: Christian Socialism, by Frederick Denison Maurice, with a pre- face by His Honour Judge Hughes, Q.C." I take it that Mr. Parker will accept such as evidence. In the preface, Mr. Hughes states: "The following Tract which we now reprint was the • first of the Tracts on Christian Socialism,' published in 1849 and the next two years. It was written by Mr. Maurice at the request of young men, chiefly law students, who were working under him in a Ragged School, and visiting the poor. Several of them had visited the Associations Ouvri‘res in Paris, which had survived the collapse of the Ateliers Nationaux in 1848. They had been much struck by the good influence of those Associations on the character of their members, and their great success from a business point of view. They proposed to make similar experi- ments in London, and had already started a Tailors' and Shoemakers' Association when this Tract appeared. The public adoption of the name 'Christian Socialism 'was made after careful consideration, and,no doubt, was the cause of much of the anger and bitter- ness with which the movement was received. Indeed, several friends who had been u orking with us pro- tested, and would never join the Society which was then founded to promote association. On the other hand the bitterness and unfairness of their attack, in the Press and on the platform, gave us at once a position with the Chartists and Socialists among the working classes which we could have got in no other way, and I have never doubted that Mr. Maurice was right. This tract was intended as a declaration of principle (or manifest as the phrase is now) to make it as clear as possible what vdre cur real meaning and objects. It answeled its purpose admirably, and I think, has a permanent value such as none other of the series of Tracts which followed it can claim, though several of these, especially Kingsley's Cheap Clothes and Nasty ' made much more noise at the time." The Tract is written in the form of "A Dialogue" between SOMEBODY (a person of respectability) and NOBODY (the writer). "SOMEBODY.— Christian Socialism! I never saw that adjective united to that substantiative before. Do you seriously believe that a Socialist can be a Christian, or a Christian a Socialist?</p>
        <p>Notionv.—l seriously believe that Christianity is the only foundation of Socialism, and that true Socialism is the necessary result of a sound Christianity. S.— Sound and true! One understands those words very well. True Socialism is your Socialism, not that of Owen, Fourier, Louis Blanc, or any other English- man, Frenchman, German. Sound Christianity is your Christianity, not that of any church, sect, school, or divine hitherto known in Christendom.</p>
        <p>N.—The Socialism I speak of is that of Owen, Fourier, Louis Blanc, and of the Englishmen. Frenc- hmen, Germans, who have fraternized with them or produced systems of their own. _ _</p>
        <p>- S.—A sifficient warrant for the other half of my proposition. Your Christianity then, I presume, is that of Owen, Louis Blanc, Fourier? A rather peculiar species of a very comprehensive genus! But to waive that point for the present. Your Socialism is that of a hundred different men at sirife with each other!</p>
        <p>N.— All these men, if 1 understand them rightly, are attempting to compass the same end. They differ about the means of compassing it.</p>
        <p>S.--The same end? Ha.ppiness, I suppose. Socialists and Anti-socialists are probably agreed so far.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>industries the control of all branches of production and exchange. This is far from the truth. Socialists recognise that such a regme would be a menace both to individual liberty and to social productivity. .Socialism seeks not merely collective ownership and control but also an organisation of labour which would be elastic, pliable, decentralised, sensitive to social need. The establishment of such a Socialist organisation of labour, necessitates a series of changes not only in the moral and intellectual order but in the political order and machinery of Government. It assumes the conquest of public powers by the organised workers (hence the usefulness of the present combination of Labourists and Socialists) and it implies the differentiation of the Government or Police State, and Industrial State.</p>
        <p>The essential difference between large limited com- panies and the State (or socially organised com- munity) is in the method of forming the controlling will. In the first case (companies) it proceeds from the meeting of shareholders. Consequently it -is inspired by self interest. In the second case (the State) it proceeds from the general body of citizens composing the State or Municipality. Therefore as the general body share in the exercise of power the general good is the inspiring motive. The first con- dition required,in order that the extension of collective ownership should be to everybody's advantage, is that the Government should belong to everybody. Hence where in more or less democratic countries the organised workers- exercise an influence there the social interest predominates in certain State monopolies, and what is to-day the exception will become the rule only when the complete conquest of public powers establishes the political emancipation of the workers. Confusion reigns to-day between the governing and the economic functions of the State, social economy and State economy. As a Socialist who believes in the government of men and the administration of things I hope to condescend to some details next week.</p>
        <p>LAZARD'S. COUNCILLOR FULTON SMITH AND OLD AGE</p>
        <p>PENSIONS. A few days ago Councillor Fulton Smith, at a meet- ing of "Shepherds" held in Warrington, was speaking on the subject of old age pensions, and in the course of his address, is reported to have said that "they all knew that the backbone of England had been the ! thrift of its inhabitants. •The more that idea was followed the better for this country instead of depend- ing upon the visionary idea of old age pensions which had no foundation whatever. He would like to draw their attention to the vagaries which had been pro- mulgated at the Trades Union Congress. Some of the leaders told them that every man and woman was to have a pension, but not one of them attempted to show the method whereby the pension was to be secured, nor how the money was to be got." These statements are remarkable, coming as they do from one of the leaders of the local Conservative party. He tells us that the idea of old age pensions Is a visionary idea, and the views promulgated by the trades union leaders at the Trades Union Congress, are vagaries. There is not much consolation in these statements for Tory trcides unionists, and other work- ing men who support the Tory party. Councillor Fulton Smith's advice to the labourers with 18s. per week is to provide for old age pensions through a friendly society. I have never yet heard of any friendly society which has a satisfactory scheme ; for providing adequate pensions to its aged members •at a reasonable age. What is to become of those who • are too poor to contribute towards the provision of old age pensions for themselves? Are they to be satisfied with what the workhouse provides? • Councillor Fulton Smith raises the old bogey and asks, where is the money to come from? I will answer that question by asking where does the money come from to provide the grant of £50,000 to Lord C'romer ? Where does the money come from to provide • a pension of £5,000 per year to Lord Nelson, who along with his ancestors have received from a generous people a sum of not less than £600,000? Where does the pension of £4,000 per year granted to Lord Peel come from? This was granted after having received a salary of £5,000 per year for eleven years. Where does the • annual pension of £4,000 granted to Mr. Gully, after receiving a salary of £5,000 per year for acting as speaker in the House of Commons come from? Where does the money come from which goes to pro- vide the numerous other pensions which are paid each year to persons who could well afkrd to do without? Can Councillor Fulton Smith tell us?</p>
        <p>Of course, this question is n-P-t- asked when pensions are granted to the well-to-do. It is only when it is propesed to grant pensions to the workers that the cry of where is the money to come from, is raised. We are told that 43 per cent. of the hardest working part of our population, however thrifty they might be, are not able to command incbmes sufficient to provide themselves with decent subsistence. How then is it possible for these to provide for old- age pensions? There are 2,000,000 of families in this country whose income is below £1 per week, and the problem is how the husband, wife and children are to be kept on that amount. The justification of asking for old age pensions is the fact that those who are to receive them are too poor during their active life to provide their own old age pensions. The country is not too poor to provide old age pensions. • In New Zealand, which is a very much poorer country than our own, they can afford to give a pension of 10s. per week to their aged workers. We are told that the annual wealth of this country is approxi- mately £1,710,000,000, and that five millions of the population receive from that sum not less than £820,000,000, whilst the remaining 38 millions receive £880,000,000.</p>
        <p>The late Chancellor of the Exchequer told the House of Commons in his last Budget speech that the capital wealth of the country was increasing by about £200,000,000 per year. This annual increase of the wealth would be sufficient to provide pensions of 5s per week to all workers having reached the age of 60 ten times over. There is plenty of wealth for old age pensions if the wealth was diverted into proper channels instead of pertnittitig a comparative few to take such a- large portion. It is estimated that one- thii d of the wealth created is taken by less than one- thirtieth of the population. • Councillor Fulton Smith advises the workers to think of these questions for themselves and not to be led by agitators. They have begun to think these ques- tions out, and one'of the results of their thinking is that they have found out there is plenty of wealth to provide pensions, but there is very little hope.of getting pensions worth having from either Tory or Liberal Governments, except they are compelled to grant them by an overwhelming public opinion. One of those who are trying to create this public opinion is THE SCOUT:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>FAMOUS PHYSICIAN Prescribes a Simple Remedy for INCREASING STRENGTH and FLESH.</head>
        <p>A FAMOUS PHYSICIAN Prescribes a Simple Remedy for INCREASING STRENGTH and FLESH.</p>
        <p>An eminent physician, who is well-kneval as a specialist and author, writes :—" No treatment for Phthisis, or weak lungs, would be complete without Dr. Cassell's Tablets. I have subjected these tablets to the</p>
        <p>severest tests and the results were of the most satis- factory kind. They increase strength and develop flesh by their power to aid assimitation. I attribute several important cures mainly to the timely use of these tablets—they have been added to my list of successful medicines."</p>
        <p>Surely tie unbiassed opinion of such a great authority, is sufficient to convince sufferers from nerve troubles, stomach and kidney disorders, thinness and weakness of the body, wrecked constitution, decline, and similar ailments, that 101 d. laid out in a box of this successful remedy would be greatly to their advantage. Any chemiets can supply Dr. Cassell's Tablets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Trip to Knutsford.</head>
        <p>Trip to Knutsford.</p>
        <p>At the invitation of Mr. John Jackson, one of the partners in the firm, who has recently been married, the staff of Messrs. E. Richardson and Son, Ltd., wholesale groders, Bridge-street, had a trip to Knuts- ford on Saturday afternoon. The party, about 3,0 in number, was accompanied by Alderman and Mrs. Bennett, Mrs. and Miss Richardson (of Padgate), and Mr. -and Mrs. Jackson. A four-horse 'bus wss engaged, and the weather being all that could be desired, the drive through the pleasant roads that lead to Knutsford was much appreciated. On arrival some participated in a game of bowls, others sauntered through the town, and viewed the several objects of interest, and had time for a walk in Tatton Park. A substantial tea was provided at the Lord Eldon Hotel, and congratulatory speeches to the donor of the day's festivities were made by Mr. Corbett, Mr. J. J. Sanderson and Mr. Bennett. The last-named referred to the associations of Mrs. Richardson's (Padgate) parents with Knutsford, they baying resided there for macy years, and especially to an uncle, who was in business in the town, .and bad been portrayed by Mrs. Gaskell in her well-known novel" Cranford," which is intended for Knutsford. It was a matter of regret that Mr. John E. Richardson and Mrs. Richard- son, who are spending a few days in Lincolnshire, were unable to be present. After tea the interval was filled with songs by members of the party, and War- rington was reached safely at ten o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Speaking to</head>
        <p>Speaking to</p>
        <p>Mothers.</p>
        <p>A grateful parent tells of her debt of gratitude to Dr. VVilliams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>"For the sake of all women br6ken in health, and particularly for the bene fit of suffering mothers, I wish to make known the debt of gratitude I owe to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People." These were the words of Mrs. E. Abrahams, residing at 29, Bell-street, Leeds and remarkable indeed is the evidence she supplies of the boon that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are to womankind. The reason is clear; not only do they supply new, good blood, but they purify bad blood, and give snpport to weary women. These pills impart strength, develop the appetite, and improve the digestion, also stimulating the functions of all the organs.</p>
        <p>For anmmia, general weakness, neuralgia, digestive troubles, and the ailments that afflict women only, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are invaluable. Mrs. Abrahams states : "It was just after the birth of my last child that I became very ill and depressed. I was so blood- less that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>I FELL AWAY TO A SHADOW.</head>
        <p>I FELL AWAY TO A SHADOW.</p>
        <p>My face was drawn, my eyes sunken, and I detested the sight of food, for even a glass of milk caused excru- ciating pains through my chest. Splitting headaches attacked me night and day, and my breathing was accompanied by stabs through my heart. If I mounted a few stairs I felt puffed. Rest' and sleep were out of the question, for when I dozed nightmares startled me. My face became quite white and my lips livid, and my nerves got so beyond control that the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>LEAST NOISE SENT TREMORS THROUGH ME.</head>
        <p>LEAST NOISE SENT TREMORS THROUGH ME.</p>
        <p>Trifles upset and worried me beyond reason and the thought haunted me that I was sinking to my grave. I had to neglect my home in many ways, because I was not strong enough for the lightest duty. I derived no benefit from ordinary medicines. I was sinking fast when Dr. Williams' Pink Pills stepped in and saved me. These blood-making pills not only saved me, but made a new woman of me.; they gave me energy I never expected to enjoy again. Asa mother speaking to mothers, I ",'-f • strongly advise Dr. Pink Pills for those weak- nesses to which they are , subject. "After a few boxes of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People my veins were full of rich, red blood. I enjoyed my meals, the pains left me, and sleep was refreshing. My friends and neighbours remarked what a wonderful change had come over me,and they congratulated me upon my clear and healthy com- plexion. I persevered with Dr. Williams' Pink Pills until As a mother. I stromrly I had been restored to advise Dr. Wlllams' Pin* perfect health. These pills Ptih for Pale Psople. are a wonderful remedy, and a great boon. To-day enjoy the best of health, and I can honestly say that I have to thank Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for it.</p>
        <p>Maid, young mother, and elderly matron everywhere sing the praises of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Thousands of the suffering sex tell gratefully hew these wonderful blood-making and nerve tonic pills cured them. Invaluable in Amemia, Indigestion, Rheumatism, Eczema and Skin Diseases, Nervous Disorders, Neuralgia, St. Vitus' Dance, Paralysh,-, and all the ailments women suffer. Sold by all dealers (but remember the substitutes some offer are utterly useless), or direct from the British Depot, 46, Holborn- viaduct, London, post free 2s. 9d., for one box. or 13s. and 9d. for six boxes. Send for an attractive illustratel booklet of cures, post free.</p>
