        <p>THE STATE.</p>
        <p>The famous French Socialist Jean Jaure's has stated that" When everybody is an official, there will be no more officials." The paradox is aimed at those who persistently say, in spite of innumerable protests, that 'collectivism has a formula "Evelything for tjle State." The object of such a cry is to create doubt by playing on the double sense of the word State. The State may be, in a broad sense the combined social organisation, but it may also be. and is generally-so understood, the police State, the instrument of official government which has for its aim the safeguarding of the interests of the propertied class. By an almost unbelievable distortion of Socialist theory it is orten said that Socialists wish to entrust to the present form of State the direction of all enterprises, the monopoly of</p>
