      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>6 6 Eee 9 9</head>
        <p>6 6 Eee 9 9</p>
        <p>MY DA _ AND NEPHEWS, _ You will remember that I had a little chat last week about working for the poor during the winter months. I know none of you really want me to give you prizes for such a good ob- ject, but I feel it would give you a little more interest in these columns to see your names as winners. Therefore, I shall try to give a nice little present to all members sending me any article for the poor during the months of November and December, and if I receive extra nice articles, I shall give extra prizes at Christmas-time to the senders. Any questions about this competition I shall be pleased to answer in these columns. It seems strange, doesn't it, to talk about Christmas-time? but now October is in, the time will quickly fly. I do hope you will help to make these presents a big success. Later on, I will tell you about some poor families I know which I should much like to help. From your loving UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>GOOD AT LISTENING. Customer: "I want some kind of a door- spring—one that won't get out of order." Hardware Dealer: "A. door-spring?" Customer: "Yes, and one that won't require the strength of an elephant to open." Dealer: "Hem 1"</p>
        <p>Customer: "And yet it must be strong enough to bring the door all the way to, and not leave it swinging open a couple of inches."</p>
        <p>Dealer: "I see:" - _ Customer: "And when the door closes I don't want it 'to ram shut like a catapult, with a crash that shakes the house from its founda- tions." ,</p>
        <p>Dealer: "Yes; You want one that will bring the door all the way to, and yet do it gently."</p>
        <p>Customer: "That's the idea. But: I don't want any complicated arrangement that re- quires a skilled mechanic to attend to it." Dealer: "No, of course not; you want some- thing simple, yet strong and effective." Customer: "That's the thing; something that can be put on or taken off easily; something that will do its work quietly, yet thoroughly, and won't be always getting out of order." Dealer: "I see. I know exactly what you want, sir—just exactly." Customer: "Well, show me one." Dealer: 'We don't keep door-springs." HARRY Copp.</p>
        <p>ROVE:</p>
        <p>Rover was a big, good-natured mastiff, and his favourite playfellow was Johnny Dale, a rosy- cheeked little boy, four years old. When Johnny had been a wee, soft mite of a baby, Rover had, been a wee, soft mite of a puppy. G , Dogs grow old much faster than children, and now Rover was quite an old dog, while Johnny was still only a little boy; but Rover was never too dignified for a frolic with his young master. He never dreamed of being separated from his beloved companion until oae dreadful evening, Johnny, all excitement, ruehed into the library where his father and mother were sitting, and Rover was lying 'on the hearthrug,</p>
        <p>"0 papa I papa!" he cried, his big blue eyes full of tears, "please, please don't sell Rover!" The astonished dog held his breath, ai.: Is- tened, harsilly able to believe his ears.</p>
        <p>"But, Johnny, you know, we are goln6 to move into the new house to-morrow," said Mr. Dale, "and we can't have Rover making dirty tracks on our new carpets with his muddy paws. If he stays outside in his kennel. we will keep him, but if he whines and scratches at the door every evening, I shall certainly have to sell him."</p>
        <p>Poor, poor Rover! He rested his rough, honest head on his paw's, and waited anxiously for Mrs. Dale to defend him, but ohe did .not speak, and all Johnny's arguments were use- less.</p>
        <p>The dog's heart—for dogs have hearts—felt as if it would break.</p>
        <p>He was not the kind of dog to whine and scratch the door!</p>
        <p>This resolution was easy to keep the next day in the hurry and bustle of moving, but when night came, and all WB6 dark and still, pour Rover felt very lonely in his new quarters. The wind howled dismally about his kennel, and his pile of straw was very dreary in com- parison with the bright library fire.</p>
        <p>It seemed as if the whole world hid dezerted him. 0 At last, unable to bear it longer. .te crept stealthily to the piazza, and peered into it- e library window. There they all were, looking so cosy and bright, and here he was, cold and lonely, pressing clotse to the window-pane. soon Johnny spied him, and ran to let him in. The dog's heart beat high with joy for a moment, and then sank down like lead, as lie heard Mrs. Dale say, "Johnny, I cannot allow Rover to come in."</p>
        <p>Rover wagged his tail meekly, and made no sound. Surely they could not complain if ha just stood quietly at the window.</p>
        <p>For a moment- there was a pause, while Mr. and Mrs. Dale looked at him with an amused smile. Then Mrs. Dale'e love for Rover ;clung the better of her love for her new carpets. she said, "I do believe that dog understood what we said last night, and it is too cruel to shut him out. Come in, you dear old fellow, come</p>
        <p>' At this welcome summons, Rover waited not an instant longer, but rushed in, jumped fran- tically about, and ended by a 'royal frolic with Johnny. From that day to this, if you go into the Dales' library any winter night, you will be sure to find Rover dozing on the hearth-rug. Be careful what you say before him, for Johnny and his mother believe that he under- stands every word he hears. D. BROWN.</p>
        <p>IDENTIFYING HIMSELF.</p>
        <p>Thirty years ago it was not a trifling matter for a traveller in France to be found abroad without. his passport. An arrest, the gnara- house, an examination by the mayor, and other , annoyances would have taught him that in ...France a foreigner should never stir out ot doors unless his passport was in one pocket and, his purse in the other. Gottschalk, the American pianist, once started, on a pedestrian tour through France. Arriving at an im, a t-te miles from Paris, he put up for the.nigat. The next morning he arose at daybreak and went out for a walk, leaving his .hand-ba, h wnieti was his passport, at the inn. He till he found himself in a large vu. and a heavy hand laid on his shoulcier. s.aur pass- port!" said the gendarme, paw,- port! It isn't with me. I leit.it at my inn uLs morning," answered the surpr.seu " Yes, yes; but if thou bast it. not, thoult march to the guard-house." Detention at the guard-house was followed by an otticial ex- amination. The mayor, a fat, good-natured man, questioned the prisoner as to his name, occupation, and motive for being in the viilage. Though the man laughed heartiiy az lhi venture, Gottschalk saw that. he was su2picious. The conversation revealed that vac two daughters of the mayor played on the piano, and that the "Bananier," one of Goltschalk's compositions, was their favourite piece. In a few minutes the composer was at 14c, with the mayor and his family for an audience. No one thought of the missing pp.eaport. for the man had identified himself. He w-au could play • as this man had played could Le none other than the young American pianist about whom all Paris was talking. When the mayor invited the musician to spend gevers: days as his guest, the gendarme was incUenant. Evii days had befallen France. he 'thought. when a stranger could enter their villag.! without a passport, and be entertained by the mayor, because he could play the piano.</p>
