      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>PETS AT SEA</head>
        <p>PETS AT SEA</p>
        <p>sailors live in a lonely world for months at e, time, it may be, and if childless women may be pardoned, for petting poodles, Jack may surely be excused for making friends with al- most any creature that will respond to his at- tentions. No wonder, suggests the "Sea Breeze," that on board ship a dog. a •goat, a pig, or a parrot is likely to find a place in the affections of homeless Jack.</p>
        <p>Dogs are, perhaps. the most favoured pets. Admiral Evans tells of a fine bull-terrier he had, which was so great a favourite with the men that they would come to the mast and ask for "shore leave" for Jowler, just ao they did for themselves.</p>
        <p>As a pet, the cat does not hold the same place as the dog, for in the traditions of the sea the cat has rather a shady reputation. Yet Mr. Bullen, in his "Log of a Sea Waif," gives a pathetic account of the way a kitten soothed his loneliness when, a boy of eleven year 6, he had his first hard experience of life at sea. There are instances of shipwrecked men risk- ing their lives to save the ship's cat; and a few yea.s ago a sailor, escaping from the Wreck of a barge off Highland Light, Massachueetts, went back to save his pet and was lost in the attempt.</p>
        <p>In one instance the pet saved the sailor. When the steamer " Wildwood " was crushed and sunk in a storm at Port Townsend, the one man on board as ship-keeper was asleep in his bunk.. The water was already in The cabin when the cat climbed to his bunk and scratched his face, and was knocked into the water for her pains. Again she scratched, and made such an outcry that the man waked just in season to escape. Bears make fine pets, and goats would be popular at sea if their omnivorous tastes were not such a serious drawback. When Captain Slocum .was sailing alone round the world in his little sloop Spray,'" Governor Sterndale, of St. Helena, gave him a goat, saying that he would be as companionable as a dog. But after the first day, and as soon as he got his " sea-legs" under him, the goat began eating everything aboard ship. The captain tied him up. and went about some work forwara, but the beast ate himself free from the rope, went into the cabin, and de- voured a fine chart of the West Indies. The next thing to go was the captain's straw hat, and the beast, after trying to eat everything, from the flying jib to the tiller rope, was sum, manly put ashore at the first landing, Ascen- sion Island.</p>
        <p>Admiral Evans, when captain of a warship, was roused one night to hear the announcement that " Dennis " had been found. Dennis, the pet pig, had mysteriously disappeared sonic days before, having, as it proved, slipped into a coal-bunker. His finding was deemed a mat- ter of sufficient moment to warrant calling the commander at midnight, and Captain Evans rose to see that the ship's cook properly cared for this famished pet. But Dennis finally died at Fortress Monroe, of eating too many white- oak acorns. H. AINTREE.</p>
        <p>An example of Cornish superstition is to be seen in a garden at Parranwell, Callestick, where there are five hives of bees draped in black crape. There has recently been a death in the family of the owners, who believe that this would have been followed by the demise of the bees had the hives not donned mourn- ing attire.</p>
        <p>• At the "Crown," Cricklewood, the public. house at which a large number of motor-omni- buses and horse 'buses stop, a robin built its nest in a gas lamp. The nesting-place is within reach of any one ,standing on the ground. At one corner of the lamp a very small piece cf glass wae broken, and through the hole the robin gained access to its new home. A family of four young ones has been reared.</p>
        <p>//WNW •</p>
        <p>" An excellent Rood, admirably adapted to the wants of infants and young persons.•• —SIR CHARLES A. CAMERON, C.EI.. M.D.. Professor of Chemastry, R.C.5.1., Medical Officer of Health for Dublin,</p>
        <p>PURVEYORS BY SPECIAL APPOINTMENT TO H.I.M. THE EMPRESS OF RUSSIA.</p>
      </div>
