      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. Leeds* Hunslet ° 2 10 Batley St. Helens* 2 1 7 Warrington Broughton R. 3 2 12 Halifax Wakefield T. Swinton* York* Ileighley* 1 1 5 Hull K. Rovers 1 0 2 Leigh* Bramley 2 3 13 Ebbw Vale* 1 3 11 Wigan Hull Oldham 4 1 11 Rochdale H 0 1 3</p>
        <p>Warrington lose at St. Helens. Warrington went to St. Helens on Saturday confident of beating the S3ints with ease,but in the end they were -vanquished by a single point. The game was completely spoiled by the constant whistling of the referee, who also crippled Warrington's game by innumerable penalties. Somehow the halves could not do the right thing for him. A lot of feeling was shown by the Warrington supporters at the course of events. The weather was very sunny and hot, and the home club had a capital crowd of about 4,000. Teams and officials : St. Helens.—Toole, back ; Bates, Hillen, J. Creevey, and C. Creevey, three-quarter backs; Drake and "Matt" Creevey, half backs; Briers, Pope, Mooney, Mavity, Mercer and J. Prescott, forwards. Warrington.—Tilley, back; Dickenson, Taylor, Isherwood, and Fish, three-quarter backs; Brooks and Lees, half hacks ; Heath, Belton, Preston, Boardman, Thomas and Harmer, forwards. Referee. Mr. Smith, Halifax. Touch judges, Messrs A. W. Gregory, Runcorn, and W. Hopwood, Liverpool. Thomas started for Warrington, and J. Prescott quickly took the ball to the visitors' quarter. Here Brooks got away and beautiful passing between him Taylor and Dickenson took the ball the length of the 'field, a try only just being saved by the home custodians. Warrington then pressed, and from a round of passing between the whole of the backs Isher- wood was tackled in possession when going through between the pasts. Warrington still persisted in aggressive tactics, and from some smart passing between Isherwood and Brooks, Taylor got over at the corner. Fish failed at goal. St. Helens now asserted themselves, and took up a footing in front of the visitors' posts, but Lees dribbled away, C. Creevey saving his side with kicking out. Brooks made a splendid individual effort to increase Wareington's lead, but Toole brought off a timely tackle. Both teams resorted to obstruction tactics, and from one shot ,at goal Creevey just failed to MO a goal. Half-time score :—Warrington, 1 try (3 points). St. Helens, nil. Pope started the second portion, and eventually Brooks and Lees opened the game out, and from passing between Isherwood and Fish, the latter ran half the length of the field and scored at the corner. Preston failed to place a goal. St. Helens went to the other end, and from a mull by Taylor on his own line Elillen succeeded in grounding the ball at the corner. C. Creevey kicked a goal. Warrington were penalised in the home half, and C. Creevey placed the ball over the cross-bar from almost three-parts of the length of the field. These unexpected reverses roused Warrington to desperate measures in the last ten minutes. Twice they got near the line, and twice more they were penalised. Fish had a shot at goal, but the ball fell under the bar, and St. Helens secured a surprise victory by 2 goals 1 try (7 points) to Warrington 2 tries (6 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A "V. ST. HELENS "A."—This match at Warrington on Saturday was distinguished by the remarkable scoring. The home team included Jenkins. Shugars and Harris' For the first few minutes the visitors held the opposition at bay, but after Harris had been pulled 'up at the corner, Kenyon dodged through and Forster landed a goal. Harris goickly followed with a try at the corner, whico Daintith failed to augment, and then followed a procession over the St. Helens line, Cartwright scoring twice in as many minutes, and equally good tries were notched by Jenkins and Kenyon. The One place kicking by Forster was particularly notable for he made seven attempts, and was successful in everp instance. Half-time score: Wurrington, seven goals eight tries (3S points) : St. Helens nil. Turning round, the home men if anything improved the pace, aud St. Helens were in hopeless difficulties, and the same order of thins prevailed, which marked the first stage. Daintith speedily opened the scoring, and Forster maintained his fine form. Cartwright raced over for another good try, and the monotony prevailed, Harris, Daintith (2), Poardman, Landor, Naylor (2), Holbrook scoring in the order their names are written. Forster added three goals, and Kenyon one. The full score was twelve goals eighteen tries (78 points) ; St, Helens, nil.</p>
      </div>
