      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>TRADERS AND RAILWAY COMPANIES.</head>
        <p>TRADERS AND RAILWAY COMPANIES.</p>
        <p>GOODS UNDER :WARR.</p>
        <p>Much inconvenience and sometimes, loss says the Birmingham Chamber of Commerce Journal, is caused to traders by reason of delay in the delivery of goods, or to their mis-delivery. In some instances the delay or mis-delivery is the result of negligence on the part of the Railway Companies' servants ; in others it is due to negligence on the part of the servants of the trader and in others it is undoubtedly the result. of insufficient direction. It is of the last we desire to say a few words. It is a very general custom to consign goods undermark and withodt any address or indication of their destination, so that if a package gots separated from its invoice, there is no information upon it to enable railway officials to know what to do with it. Everyone having the slightest acquaintance with the immense volume of traffic, involving thousands of separate con- signments,passing daily over the railway system, will realise the possibility of packages going astray, and the consequent difficulty of getting them to their proper station owing to the want of address. There are of course substantial reasons for the practice of consigning goods undermark, but it is worth while con- sidering whether, whilst maintaining this practice, it is not possible in a large number of cases, to give some more distinctive indication of the destination of the goods. If it is, then we believe that it will not only facilitate handling by the Railway Companies, but save the trader considerable trouble and complaint from his customers. The suggestion we have to make is that in every possible case consignments sent under mark should also be marked with the name of the town of destination, thus—B Oxford. We believe this would be elcomed by the Railway Companies and that if it were generally adopted it would greatly facilitate the dis- tribution and delivery of traffic. We shall be glad to hear the views of our members with reference to this suggestion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>KEEP ZAM-BUK HANDY! Daily Mishaps Make it a Daily Need.</head>
        <p>KEEP ZAM-BUK HANDY! Daily Mishaps Make it a Daily Need.</p>
        <p>Mother and Child Cured.</p>
        <p>The far-seeing and economical housewife is recognising more and more the great necessity of always keeping a box of Zam-Buk in the house ready for any emergency. One never knows before the day's work is ended what is going to happen in the way of small accidents— the children falling and grazing their knees or cutting their hands, the housewife cutting, burn- ing or scalding her fingers, the husband return- ing from his day's work with a lacerated wound.</p>
        <p>In all these and similar cases all that is needed is to clean the wound in tepid water and apply a little Zam-Buk on a piece of clean linen. All fear of complication or blood poisoning may be banished. The germs having been killed off, the wound will heal up in Nature's own way with the aid of the rich herbal juices present in Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>* * * *</p>
        <p>Apart from accidents, however, Zam-Buk is a specific for piles, bad legs, ulcers, eczema, and all forms of skin diseases. Mrs. Sarah Pool, of 29, Station-road, Hounslow, Middlesex, .ar; "some months ago My right ankle began to ache and swell very badly, and soon afterwards a tiny sore made its appearance. It troubled me very much at nights, and the pain was so intense I could get no sleep.. In a few days I was unable to bear any pressure on the right foot, and I had to wear a large slipper. I used doctor's lotion in vain,and, being now in desperate straits, I tried Zam-Buk. * * * " I bathed the leg and applied Zam-Buk freely on a cloth two or three times a day. When •I took off the bandage a second time I noticed that the wound was discharging. This occurred more or less for two or three days, after which, pain was less, and the swelling in the ankle was down. As I kept on with Zam-Buk, I continued to improve very rapidly, and soon the pain left me altogether. In three weeks the wound was quite healed. I am now quite well, and have never suffered the slightest with my leg since. "My daughter, Annie, aged four, had a bad attack of scalp disease. Her head became a mass of running sores. I had such faith in Zam- Puk that I applied it night and day over the affected parts.. In a few days the discharge stopped, and the s ,res dried. Now the childss scalp does not show the slightest trace of any skintrouble. Her head, thanks to Zam-Buk, is free from dandruff, and her hair has grown quite thick. I shall always be pleased to recommend this grand healing balm to anyone who suffered like we did." * * *</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk, the world's greatest and only perfect healing balm, should be in every home. No ordinary ointment, cheap salve, or liniment, con- taining coarse animist fat and mineral poisons, cm lay claim to such healing, soothing and germ-killing qualities. Zain-Bnk is superior .to these things because the rich herbal medicines which it contains soak into the pores of the skin at onc, destroying disease germs, averting blood poisoning after a brnise or cut, allaying pain and inflammation, and growing new, healthy skin where it has been destroyed by sores or ulcers. Of all chemists, at Is. I Id. or 2s. 9d. per box (2s. 9d. box contains nearly four times the Is. and is admirable for family use). Zarn-Buk is regarded by its regular users as worth its weight in gold. Have you tested it yet ?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>QUICK ROUTE TO THE CONTINENT.</head>
        <p>A QUICK ROUTE TO THE CONTINENT.</p>
        <p>Still pursuing their forward policy the Great Central Railway have not been content to let their Continental service linger behind that of any of its competitors. This year has marked a new era in the service between the Fl umber and Continental Ports by the addition of two fine Tlrbine Steamers, each of 2.000 tons, the " Marylebone "arid the " Imtningham " to the fleet which have bean placed upon the Rotterdam Service, and commencing, in October an entirely new service of Continental trains will be run from the North of England in conndction with these boats. On Tuesdays, Thursdays and Saturdays a Corridor Express with .Restaurant Car attached will leave Liverpool (Central) at 3.30 p.m., Manchester(Central) at 4.22 p.m., Sheffield at 5.31 p.m., and Betford at 6.3 p.m., for Grimsby Docks, where the Turbine boat leaves for Rotterdam at 7.20 p.m. Connections are also given at Sheffield with the L4O p.m. express from London (Marylebone). Leicester and Nottingham • and at Retford with the 4.10 p.m. from York, which also brings passetigers from Edinburgh, Glasgow, Newcastle, aed Leeds. At Sheffield connections are also given with Bradford, Halifax and Huddersfield. In the reverse direction a new Continental express serving the places above mentioned leaves Grimsby docks at 11.30 a.m. on Wednesdays, Fridaxs and Sundays, and awaits the arrival of the Turbine steamer leaving Rotterdam the night before. The special feature of this service is that it enables business men to leave the North of England for the Continent quite an hour later than by any other existing routs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>An Habitbal Drunkard.</head>
        <p>An Habitbal Drunkard.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Court on Saturday Charles Naylor 27, Eldonestreet, appeared on a warrant to show cause why a separation order should not be granted to his wife on the grobnd that he was an habitual drunkard.— Complainant said prisoner catne home drunk repeatedly. He turned the children out, and broke several panes of glass in the kitchen and scullery.—ComplaiDant's son said prisoner had been drunk five 'times in four days.— Prisoner said he would be all right if he could let drink alone.—A separation order was granted, defendant to pay his wife 10s. a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>AN IMPRON'ENIENT.</head>
        <p>AN IMPRON'ENIENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Tvviss having agreed to the removal of the wall at the corner of Mersey-street and Academy- street on conditions that it be replaced on the agreed line by a suitable wall and palisade; the Street Improvement Committee resolved that the offer be agreed to. Alderman BENNETT moved, and Councillor EVANS seconded, the confirmation of the minutes. . . .</p>
        <p>Councillor CLEG G, alluding to the above minute, said the removal of the wall would be a great boon to those who had to conduct the traffic ' tbrdugh these two important streets. He considered that the offer which had been made was a generous one on the part of the trustees of the Academy-street mission. (Hear, hear.) The minutes were carried. EARLY CLOSING FOR HAIRDRESSERS.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee reported that the Town Clerk having submitted a draft order with reference to the proposed closin,, of hairdressers' and barbers' shops it was resolved, that the same be approved and the neces- sary advertisement given in accordance with the Act. The proposed order provides that all barbers' shops throughout the borough of Warrington shall be closed for serving customers and for shaving or dressing the hair of a customer, at the hours on the several days of the week following ;—Sunday, Good Friday and Christmas Day, all day ; at 8.30 p.m on Monday; at 8. 30 p.m. on Tuesday ; at 9 p.m. on Wednesday; at 1 p.m. on Thursday; at 9.30 p.m. on Friday ; at 10.30 p.m. on Saturday (except on those days imme- diately preceding Christmas Day, Good Friday and Warrington Walking Day, when the hour of closing shall be optional up to 10.30 p.m. ; except on Bank Holidays, when the hour of closing shall be optional up to 12.15 p.m.; and except on Warrington Walking Day, when the hour of closing shall be optional up to 10 a.m.) Where any trade or business other than that of a barber or is hairdresser is carried on in a barber's shop, the shop may be kept open after the closing hour for the purposes of such other trade or business alone. Notwithstanding anything in this Order a barber may shave or dress the hair of a customer after the olosing hour, at the residence or office of the customer. If any person contravenes the provisions of this Order he will be liable, on conviction under the Summary Jurisdiction Acts to a fine, not exceeding in the case of the first offence one pound, in case of the second offence five pounds, in case of a third or sub- sequent offence twenty pounds, provided that nothing in this Order shall render any person liable to a penalty for serving a customer, or for shaving or dressing the hair of any customer who was in the shop before the closing hour. Councillor EvAxs, in moving the confirmation of the minutes, explained that what was being done now was only the preliminary step to the taking out of the order which would have to be advertised twice in a news- paper or newspapers, advertised by placard in the borough and four weeks allowed for objections to be made on the part of those interested in the business. The committee were bound to consider thoss objections and they were not precipitatin,, the matter in any way, but simply complying with the legal formalities. Councillor BURTON seconded but siid there was a certain amount of opposition to the Order and the com- mittee should proceed cautiously. Councillor ASHCROFT asked for a draft of the Act. The TOWN CLERK: I should be pleased to lend you one.</p>
        <p>Councillor Asficrtorr : But cannot each member of the Council have one? (Laughter.) The Tows CLERK replied th.tt he could not do that. Further questioned by the Councillor he said that two- thirds of thefiairdressers must be in favour of the pro- posal to close. Alderman Tissres said that if the order were generally applied, it might press hardly in some cases. Some hairdressers did considerable busidess on Thurs- day afternoon as social functions were often held in the evening. It would be a great hardship to those who shaved to have to go to a ball with a rough, stubbly chin. (Laughter.) At this stage of the meeting the Mayor hnt'. to leave to fulfil an engagement, and Alderman Pierpoint was voted to the chair.</p>
        <p>In answer to Councillor Steel, The TOWN CLERK said every hairdresser in the town would be served with a notice. Councillor Dr. JOSEPH : Will certain members be exempt at the di scrqion of the committee? The TOWN CLERk : Oh no. If you consent to the order it applies to everyone in the town carrying on the business.</p>
        <p>In snswer to Councillor Pemberton, the TOWN CLERK said the Council could limit the area to which the order would apply. Councillor PEMBERTON :</p>
        <p>You can leave a certain</p>
        <p>street out? The TOWN CLERK : Yrs.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILDING : I think not. We have op Act of Parliament to deal with ; not a.byelaw. I say you cannot exempt a street, no matter %tat the 1 own Clerk says. The 'rows COMIC : With all respect to Councillor Wilding, and putting a side his impertinence, I say the Town Council have power to limit the Order to a particular area. Councillor EVANS said that after the Council had confirmed the resolution that night and notice bad been given, the question would largely depend on the hair- dressers themselves, because they would have to com- municate to the committee the nature of their objection to the order. being made. After due notice had expired and time given for the objections to be banded in the committee, be understood, would proceed to formulate • the order. If the hairdressers wished a certain area to be excluded, they should make representations to that effect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>SLAUGHTER-HOUSES AND ABATroras.</head>
        <p>SLAUGHTER-HOUSES AND ABATroras.</p>
        <p>'The Health Committee reported that a deputation from the Co-opPrative Societ) waited upon the com- mittee with reference to The proposed erection of a slaughter-house in Lovely-lane. The deputation having explamed their views, withdrew oa the uudersta nding</p>
        <p>THE TRAM DOORS. A COMPLAINT.</p>
        <p>Councillor STEEL drew the Tramway Committee's attention to the unsatisfactory state of the doors on the tops of some of the tram cars on the Cemetery route. Some of them would not fasten, and when the car wobbled on the Manchester-road tbe doors flew open. It was very inconvenient, especially in the winter time, to have to get up to close the door. Councillor WILKINSON promised that the matter should be attended to.</p>
        <p>The minutes were passed. INCREASE OF SALARY.</p>
        <p>The General Purposes Committee recommended that Mr. A. Starkey's salary, as registration clerk, be Increased from £llO to .£125 per annum. The minutes were adopted on the motion of the i CHAIRMAN, seconded by Alderman Trg:grox.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>APOLLO PRIZE CHOIR.</head>
        <p>APOLLO PRIZE CHOIR.</p>
        <p>Annual Concert in the Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Apollo Prize Male , Choir are to be complimented upon the excellence of their annual concert which took place in the Parr Halt on Monday evening. One would like to have seen a larger attendance of the public,for although the higher- priced seats were well filled there was plenty of room in the popular parts. However, the audience was appreciative and the high-class programme was received with unmistakable approval. The choir, of course,took a prominent part in the entertainment. It is an encouraging sign of the times that such a numerous body of men can be found to devote themselves enthusiastically to musical culture and there is every indication that the choir continues to make headway. Of the singing on Monday it is due to speak in terms of high praise. Under Mr. Harry Berrey's able conductorship, the various items received adequate interpretation, the voices being well balanced, while the attention to expression was effective in a marked degree. The opening chorus " Where is He ?" from Beethoven's" Mount of Olives "was particularly fine, the contrast between the pathetic appeals of the disciples and the furious cries of the mob being well • brought out. "The Destruction of Gaza" (De Rille) gave the resources of the choir full scope, the volume of sound being highly impressive. An old favourite " Crossing the Plains" (Price) was given with splendid dramatic spirit. Two choice part songs were " Myfanwy " (Harry Evans) and "The Soldier's Fare- well" (Hatton)and these gems of harmony delighted the audience, woo gave vent to their appreciation with warm applause. The Committee were fortunate in their choice of artistes and not one failed to please—indeed the concert went with a delightful swing throughout. Miss M. Williams possesses a sweet soprano voice of pleasing compass and she infused feeling into her rendering of " The Rosary "(Nevin). Her other songs —" Where is another sweet as my sweet " (Sullivan) and " Should he upbraid ? " (Bishop) were interpreted with acceptable piquancy. Madame Bertenshaw is a contralto of repute and fully justified the high opinions formed of her by the charming way in which she sang the pretty compositions In the time of Roses " (Corbett) " Wake up, little darkie " (Ciutsain), " The sweeetest flower that blows" (Hawley) and" An old world serenade " (Halmund). Mr. Emlyn Davies, the well-known baritone, afforded the audience a rich treat. He sang When I survey " (rettinan) with a depth of feeling in keeping with the solemnity of the work, and nis later efforts "The Lowland Sea " (Bransconibc) and "My old Shako " (Troterc) , were given in a breezy, yet thoroughly artistic style. Mr. Davies 's perfect enunciation was a notable feature. Mr. John Paley, the champion cornet soloist of the world, is familiar to Warrington concert goers and his fine performances once more evidenced his wonderful mastery of the instrument and his real musical talent. The audience would like to,.have heard more selections from him, but he pleaded to be excused from respond- ing to the hearty encore, because he was suffering from neuralgia. Mr. F. M. Darby, organist of the Warring- ton Parish Church, played two pianoforte solos "Impromptu in A fiat " (Chopin) " Elincettes " (Moszkowski) with taste and skill, his command of technique being admirable. The humour of the evening was supplied by Mr. Harry Lawton who was eminently successful in his efforts to amuse. His impersonations were exceptionally clever. Mr. W. Pearce played the accompaniments very creditably.</p>
