      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor</p>
        <p>a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must be written on one side only of the paper and sent in, if possible,not ater than Thursday. " A SUFFERER" writes to support the efforts of "J. R. Griffiths" " Demos" and " Examiner " in ventilating the evil of drunkenness in \Varrington. He draws attention to the number of Women's Clubs held on licensed premises in the town, and says no effort should be spared to suppress them. We regret that pressure on our space this week prevents us printing the letter more fully.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Forthcoming Temperance Legislation A Real Time Notice.</head>
        <p>Forthcoming Temperance Legislation A Real Time Notice.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Much interest is being displayed at the present time amongst all classes of the community as to the reforms likely to be included in the Bill which the Government has promised shall be the first important measure of the next Session of Parliament.. .</p>
        <p>Frequent references are being made in the Press to the enactment of a time limit to the compensation clauses of the Act of 1904 but the particular nature of the time limit required does not seem to be indicated. It is important that the precise nature of the limit or notice should be clearly defined. A mere provision that, at the end of a specified time, all claims for com- pensation should cease and the position become what it was prior to 1904 will not meet the case at all. What is required in the present involved state of our Licens- ing Laws is the enactment of a satisfactory time "Notice" which shall not be confined only to the com- pensation clauses of the 1901 Act, but shall include a provision that at the expiration of the " Notice "all licences shall be regarded as new licences and be granted only on payment of the full monopoly value. This would put the State in full possession of the monopoly which it has itself created and clear the way for further constructive reforms. Thoughtful considera- tion of the subject will make it abundantly clear that herein lies the key to the situation ; and that this is the main reform upon which the whole energies of the temperance party should be concentrated at the present time. When once the State resumes possession of the monopoly value a great advance in the history of the temperance movement from the legislative standpoint will have been effected.—Yours, etc., OBSERVER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>MAYORALTY. Alderman Smethurst Again Accepts Office.</head>
        <p>THE MAYORALTY. Alderman Smethurst Again Accepts Office.</p>
        <p>----- The announcement will be received° with general satisfaction that Alderman Smethurst has accepted the unanimous invitation of the Town Council to continue in office as Mayor for another year. His Worship's decision was made known at the Council meeting on Tuesday evening and called forth warm applause. When the meeting was half over, The MAYOR asked for permission to leave to fulfil an engagement out of town. He had hoped the business would have been got through in 20 minutes, but as it had not been he must beg to be excused. Alderman BENNETT said that at a caucus meeting the Council unanimously agreed to invite the Mayor to accept office for another year, and a committee was appointed to wait upon him. They did so a few days ago, and the Mayor had given the matter his best con- sideration in the meantime. He (Alderman Bennett) was glad ta be able to tell the Council that Alderman Smethurst had promised to accept office again. (Applause.) There was only one condition, and that was that they would accord him the same loyal support in the future that had been given to him in the past. (Hear, hear.) Councillor EVANS : We will do it. (Applause.) Alderman BEN-NETT said the Mayor had conducted the business of the Council with s geniality and sense of fair play that everyone had appreciated, and the whole town would be pleased to hear of his decision. The MAYOR said he thanked the Council for their appreciation of his services, and if he only had the help and assistance he had already bad he would have no difficulty at all in conducting the business of the Council during the next 12 months. He had not told a single soul his decision—not even his wife—until he came to the Council meeting that evening and saw Alderman Tinnion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>HOW TO USE THE GARVEN-PLACE SITE. Baths, Bowling Green or Municipal Offices ?</head>
        <p>HOW TO USE THE GARVEN-PLACE SITE. Baths, Bowling Green or Municipal Offices ?</p>
        <p>A discussion arose at the Town Council meeting on Tuesday with reference to the Garven-Place site. __</p>
        <p>At their meeting on August 29th, the Town Hall Parks and Baths oommitte3 resolved that the Borough Surveyor prepare plans for new baths and for the pro- posed bowling green to be constructed on the land behind Garven-place, and that the members of the com- mittee meet the Finance and Estate Committee, and request them to hand over sufficient land for the pro. posed new baths and bowling green. The Finance and Estate Committee at their meeting on September 2nd received the Chairman of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee and Councillors Ellison, Evans and Benson who attended with regard to the land which that committee had applied for behind Garven-place for the extension of the Baths and the construction of a bowling green. Resolved, "That before any part of the land be appropriated to the above purpose, the Borough Surveyor be instructed to prepare a complete and comprehensive scheme for the erection of new Munci pal Offices, and to report as to the beet use to which the land can be applied." Alderman BENNETT, in moving the confirmation of the minutes of the Finance Committee,said some people appeared to think their decision was intended to obstruct the provision of a bowling green, but that was the last thought in the minds of the committee. Personally, he was in favour of a bqwling green in each ward, as well as all the baths they could provide. but it was felt that some day they would have to build a. complete block of municipal offices, and make develop- ments here. It was, therefore, felt that they ought to have a free hand, and not prejudice themselves against the carrying out of a big scheme in the future. The resolution did not commit them to the immediate erection of the municipal offices. Alderman FORSHAW saconded. Councillor PEMBERTON spoke in favour of a bowling green, which would certainly pay its way by the time an extension of the Town Hall was wanted. There was no reason to defer the laying of the green. Councillor EvaNs said he was not a bowler, hut he believed in the recreation, and that municipalities should provide what was necessary for recreation. If the green was not made this winter there would be no bowling on it next summer. Alderman BENNETT said the resolution was worded more emphatically than he had intended. He did not contemplate the immediate building of municipal offices. What they were doing was simply feeling their way.</p>
        <p>The minutes were then agreed to. Upon the minutes of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee coming up for consideration. Councillor PEmmittoN drew attention to the following resolution: —" That the Borough Surveyor prepare plans for new baths and for the pr3posed bowling green to be con- structed on the land behind Garven-place." If the Surveyor, he said, was going to prepare two sets of plans, one for the baths and one for the bowling green, that would be all right, but there was a grave objection to the two being brought together. The Collodi should have them independently so that they could accept or reject either of them. Councillor EvANs said the resolution referred more particularly to the land that would be required for the purposes. Councillor PEMBERTON argued that if the Council intended building further swimming baths they should be in Howley or Lovely-lane, or some other part of the town. not Garven-place. If the present baths required enlarging there was land available adjoining the build- ing. lie was afraid that by coupling the two schemes, the bowling green project might be hindered. Alderman BENNETT pointed out that while they had made provision for the schoolboys to learn to swim they had neglected the girls altogether. The girls were just as much entitled to acquire that useful and healthy art as the boys. Councillor WiLKINiON said the intention was simply to ascertain bow much land would be required. He should object to the buildiqg of municipal offices and he thought part of the site might be reserved for remunerative shop property. Conncillor BARTON moved an amendment that the minute be referred back in order that the committee could state exactly what they desired. Councillor Storr spoke in favour of reduced prices for the baths to enable working men to take advantage of them. He would like baths nearer the schools and the homes of the workers,and suggested one on the open space off Orford-lane. Councillor STEEL seconded the amendment, and said the only question was whethor the plans should show both baths and bowling green or be separate and distinct.</p>
        <p>_ Councillor PEMBERTON thought the matter ought not to he delayed, even a month, if they wanted a bowling green for next season. The town was woefully behind in bath accommodation. In the large bath the swimmers were like herrings in a barrel, while the ladies bath would not hear talking about. Workmen ought to he able to have a bath for id., as in Liverpool for the pest forty years: Councillor EVANI3 said the discussion was out of order, as the Borough Surveyor was instructed on September 3rd to prepare a scheme for the erection of a new bath and the laying out of a bowling green. Councillor LORD and Alderman BENNETT spoke in favour of the amendment, the latter observing that it 'was illogical to proceed with the plans until they knew what land was available. The amendment was carried by an overwhelming majority.</p>
        <p>For Chronic Chest Complaints, Woods' Great reppermiut Cure. 1/11, 219.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>MR. C. E. PARKER AND THE SMOKE</p>
        <p>NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>Spirited Criticism of the Health Committee.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Beautiful Warrington Society was held in the Cu-operative ETall, Sankey-st., on Thursday evening, under the presidency of Canon Morley Stevenson. Amongst those present were : Alderman Bennett, Councilor Wilson, Messrs. C. E. Parker, C. Madeley, E. J. Hall, W. Silcoek, J. Fairhurst, J. Longland (hon. treasurer), and G. H. Smith (secretary).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>WORK OF THE YEAR.</head>
        <p>THE WORK OF THE YEAR.</p>
        <p>The annual report stated that the Society continued to expand, and now had about 360 members. The figures were certainly encouraging, but the Committee felt that in a town of the size and importance of War- rington this number should be greatly exceeded. A litele more individual effort amongst the members generally would do much in this direction. After referring to the tree planting and City Beautiful move- ments the report stated that during the year great progress hsd been made with the Garden Suburbs scheme. A Company had been-formed and land purchased, and a Tenants' Society, which would primarily deal with the leasing of the land and the building of houses, was in active course of formation. These societies, of course, existed quite independently of the Beautiful Warrington Society, but it was hardly necessary to add that the movements had their entire sympathy and support, and, in fact, were allied with the general objects of the Society. The question of smoke abatement continued to be one of the phases of interest of the Society. A highly interesting paper on the subject was read by Dr. Markel at the first Garden City Conference in Man- chester. This would shortly be published in pamphlet form, and in the meantime the Secretary would be happy to supply anyone with a print of Dr. Markel's paper. There was, no doubt,a very great deal to be done in this connection, and, to quote from the paper, "In accom- plishing the task our greatest difficulty is to move the dead weight of public indifference. Probably there never was an evil of such magnitude about which there was such general apathy." Practical remedies for the abate- ment of the evil were suggested by the writer of the paper. The second annual exhibition of flowers and plants was held in the Drill Hall on the 31st August, and was very successful. The School Childrea's Branch con- tinued to be a very important element of the work of the Society. The juvenile section had over 2,500 mem- bers, and some splendid efforts, which must bear good fruit, were being put forth by the teachers at the various schools. The thanks of the Committee were also due to Mr. F. H. Longshaw, the hon. secretary of the Children's Branch, who performed excellent services in connection with this branch. It was extremely satisfactory to observe the enthusiasm of the children and the interest they took in rearing their plants. In September last Mr. J. Fairhurst, who had been hon. Secretary to the Society from its inception, intimated that through business pressure he would have to relinquish the office. Very great regret was expressed by the Committee, and thanks were tendered for his invaluable servioes. Mr. J. Ryan very kindly undertook to act as Secretary pro tem., and performed the duties most successfully until June last, when through pressure of business he was unable to continue in the office. A vote of thanks to him was passed by the Committee. It was then decided to advertise for a secretary and the present secretary was appointed on the 19th June, 1907. It might safely then be claimed that the society was making good progress, and thanks were due to those who had in various ways assisted in carrying on the work, and if a little extra individual effort on the part of the members could be secured there was no doubt that the scope and usefulness of the Society would be much enlarged. The balance sheet was submitted and showed a balance due to the Treasurer of £2O 15s. 9d.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN,in moving the adoption of the report, said the membership was in one sense satisfactory, but he hoped they were not going to rest content. The great want of the Society was a little more missionary enthusiasm and zeal. With regard to their work, they might speak very hopefully. There was no doubt that a decided improvement had taken place through the efforts of the Society in the town. (Hear, hear.) They were much indebted to the Mayor who had always been a warm friend of the movement and they were all glad he had accepted office for another year.(Applause.) The work amongst the children—and they had particu- larly to thank Mr. Longsnaw, the secretary of that section—was most encouraging and he trusted they were implanting in the rising generation a love of the beautiful of which they would see the fruits in years to come. There was one thing of which he could not speak so hopefully and that was the important question of smoke abatement. He was afraid they had really nothing to chronicle this year that was hopeful in that direction. He understood that all the new works which had been put up in the neigh- bourhood of Manchester owing to the boom in cotton were practically smokeless. Unfortunately, however, that did not touch the old works, and the expense, he supposed, of converting them into smokeless works was very great and people could not always see their way to do it. The Society must do the best it could to influence public opinion and to show conclusively that it was not only a great boon to the community that they should rid the air of the dark clouds of smoke which oppressed us now, but also that it would be a financial gain to those who adapted their works to the new appliances. Referring to the change in the secretaryship, Canon Stevenson paid a high tribute to the untiring energies of Mr. Fairhurst. He was sure that in Mr. Smith they had a capable secretary who would always act in the best interests of the Society. Canon Stevenson touched upon two by- products of the Society—the Garden Suburbs movement and the City Beautiful Conference, and as indicating how the movement for beautifying towns was spreading he said he had received letters inquiry from Leeds, Sheffield, different parts of London, and even from America. Their movement was no mere fad, but a real God-inspired work, that they should try to make the homes of the people cleanly, healthy and beautiful (Applause.) MR. PARKER AND THE SMOKE NUISANCE. RIGOROUS MEASURES ADVISED.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. PARKER seconded, and said the paragraph in the report with regard to the smoke nuisance, put the matter very mildly. The question of smoke abate- ment was vital to the prosperity of the Society. (Hear, bear.) So long as flowers, plants, buildings, clothes and faces were smeared over by soot, he did not think the Society could prosper as it onght to do, or tbat the townspeople could be as happy as they ought to be. He knew that the Society desired to effect an improve- ment by spreading information, and by moral suasion rather than by force. He did not think the Society should be expected to put the law into operation, although it was quite within the power of the Society or members of it to appeal to the Local Government Board if the authorities did not do their duty, and if the Board were assured that that duty was not being done. they would take the matter up. The burgesses had elected gentlemen to the rown Council who had in trust the health and well-being of the people, and the duty was with them. They had elected a Health Committee whose work was to look after this special department and see that the rights of the community in regard to any nuisance were respected. Personally he had a very great respect for the gentlemen in the Council who gaue their time, attention and energy, without reward, to the affairs of the town. But his respect and thanks could only be expected if those representatives did their duty, and he did not think the Health Committee were doing their duty in regard to the smoke nuisance. Their work, if they had done anything, was weak, feeble and ineffective. From his experience of Warrington during the last twenty-five years, it seemed to him that the nuisance instead of improving had become worse, and whiie other towns such as Bolton, Manchester, Leeds, Edi nburgh,and Glasgow had been exercising their powers under the Health Act of 1875, and abating the nuisance, Warrington had practicably done nothing. He did not really know wherein the blame lay, in regard to the Health Committee. He did not know whether it was, as somebody had suggested to him—he did not know who were on the Health Committee, so he was not speaking personally—that a good many of the members of the Committee were interested in works and that many of them were afraid to take action because of their position. He would not like to think that was so. He thought the Warrington authorities desired to do their duty and would do it. An explanation was wanted from the Health Committee by the people of Warrington on account of their inactivity in this matter. The state of the town in regard to smoke was a very serious reflection on those who had the matter in trust. He heard somebody say that on this question not one of the members, with the solitary exception of Alderman Bennett, "had the pluck of a louse." (Laughter.) He believed that if the question were brought before the manufacturers in a pointed and distinct way by the authorities they would do their best to co-operate. But the matter was not so brought. It was largely due to indifference. Dr Market's paper and other information was very con- vincing and interesting, but he did not think it got into the proper hands. Many manufacturers lived out- side and did not notice the personal inconvenience, and when they came into town other things engaged their attention. He did not think any reasonable manu- facturers would resent the authorities putting reason- able pressure upon them to make the town cleaner and brighter. (Hear, hear.) There was too much fear and timidity on the part of the Society and of the authorities. Every reasonable manufacturer ought to respect the rights of the people who were obliged to live in Warrington, and as far as the unreasonable manufacturers were concerned, there was no use arguing I with them except by means of summonses and flnes.l It might be necessary for the Society to proceed in a , mild way but he did not think much good would be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>WARRINGTON SWIMMING CLUB. Annual Gala.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SWIMMING CLUB. Annual Gala.</p>
        <p>The crowded attendance at the Corporation Baths, Legh-street, on Thursday was convincing evidence that a great deal of interest is taken locally in swimming. The annual gala of the Warrington Club is a popalar event, and the programme on Thursday was such as to enhance the popularity gained in the past. There was a capital entry of athletes for the various events, amongst them being T. Pemberton, who up to Thursday night was the holder of the 150 yards Championship of War- rington. He was beaten by R. Allen by about five yards in the record time of main. 50secs., last year's time being lmin. 51secs. Great praise is duo to the following officials for the way in which the arrangements were carried out Judges: Messrs. E. Draper, C. Ashton, and C. H. Wood. Handicappers for local events, the whole of the Committee ; handicappers for Open Events, L. and D. Handicapping Board; starter, Mr. F. R. Edwards; timekeeper, Mr. J.F. Finnegan ; competitors' stewards, Messrs. E. Dell. R. T. Harding and S. Roberts; stewards, 3/Tesoro. J. Newton, C. H. Wool, G. Anderton, F. Garside, A. Barlow, D. Wright and C- Woods. Special mention must be made of Mr. J. N. Vernon, who ably discharged the duties of hon. secretary, and Mr. S. Walker, the efficient hon. treasurer of the Club. CHAIRMAN'S ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>During an interval the Mayor (who presided) gave a short address.He said it was no use for him to talk about swimming because he did not know anything whatever about it. Still it was a grand thing to learn swimming and he regretted not learning how to swim in his boy- hood. People were entitled to be proud of having learned the natatory art. He was sure a great amount or interest had been displayed of late in swimming in Warrington, and he was delighted to see such a fine exhibition that evening. There was not much profit derived by the Corporation from the baths but he hoped that in about another year they would have the baths which there had been so much discussion about in Garven-place. (Applause.) He thought young people received a great amount of encouragement from such exhibitions of swimming as tkey had seen that night and he would conclude by saying that he had to thank the officials on behalf of his wife and himself for their kind invitation. (Applause.) THE RESULTS.</p>
        <p>50 YARDS Local.. JUNIOR HANDICAP.-First heat: 1, R. Beaman :2, L. Leaity. Second heat: I,C. Taylor; 2. A. Kay. Third heat: 1, W. Cooper; 2, H. Baker. Fourth heat: 1, H. Hawthorn; 2, J. Sutton. Fifth heat: 1, H.Horabin ;2, P. Delaney. Sixth heat : 1, G. Bowcock ;2, J. Sanders. Seventh heat : 1, W. Miller; 2, H. Hazeldine. Eighth heat: 1, W. Jolly; 2, R. Weir and W. Downing dead heat. First and second in each heat had to swim in the final, which produced a good struggle for supremacy. The winner was W. Cooper, who came in first by about three yards; 2, J. Sutton,. 3, H. Hazeldine ; 4, R. Beaman. FINAL WORKS SQUADRON CHAMPIONSHIP.-Pearson and Knowles (Holders) v. Co-operative Stores. This race caused much excitement in the baths and outside. Co-operative Stores were the favourites and after lead- ing nearly all the way each time ran out winners by about a yard admidst applause. FIFTY YARDS SCHOOLBOY CHAMPIONSHIP OF WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT.-Tll order to save time both heats in this race were swum in one. Final: 1, R. Atkinson (holder) ; 2, R. Chalmers; 3, S. Rober ts; all of Warrington British. Time 31 2-ssecs. 150 YARDS CHAMPIONSHIP OF WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT.-An exceedingly great amount of interest was taken in this race, T. Pemberton being the holder. Pemberton was leading in the first two lengths by a very narrow margin but was speedily overtaken by R. Allen who won a grand race by a few yards. The time was a record, 1 min. 50 secs., Pemberton doing the 150 yards in 1 min. 51 secs. last year. The following are the prizes for this race :-First prize, Licensed Victuallers' Perpetual Challenge Trophy and Gold Medal (presented by the Warrington and District Licensed Victuallers' Association). 2nd and 3rd prizes, gold medals. 100 YARDS OPEN HANDICAP.-First heat: 1, H. Bradbury; 2, H. Bowcock. Second heat: 1, J. Ho warth; 2, J. Dwyer. Third heat: 1, W. Sutton; 2, G. M. Mulligan. Fourth heat : 1, R. Lucas, Osborne]; 2; T. Prince, Warrington. Fifth heat: 1, J. Ravenscroft, Salford; 2, J. Peers, Widnes. Sixth heat: 1, J. Aylward, Pendleton ; 2,H. Turner Warrington. Seventh heat: 1, P. Higham ;2, A. Smith, both of Warrington. In the final J. Ravenscroft (Salford), came in first closely followed by W. Sutton (Warrington), 2, H. Bradbury (Salford), 3. The winner's time was lmin. Bsecs. First prize, marble striking clock and pair of bronzes; 2nd, 18-carat gold-filled Watham watch; 3rd, gent's dressing case ; fastest loser, barometer. 50 YARDS LOCAL LADIES' HANDICAP :-Miss Grounds was the first to get away in this race and retained the lead until nearly the end, when she was overtaken by Miss Barlow end Miss Baker. Result :- 1, Barlow; 2, Baker; 3, Grounds. Time. 40 3-ssecs. The following are the prizes for this race :-First prize, lady's dressing case ; second, silver photo frame ; third, silver-plated jam dish. At this stage Mr. John Ashton was warmly applauded for a capital exhibition of life-saving. COMIC EVENT.-This event caused very much amuse- ment, the competitors having to swim in costumes and start from the shallow end of the bath with a pair of trousers, swim to the board in the centre of the bath, get on the hoard and put the trousers on, fasten them, swim to the deep end and back to the board, get on to board again, take the trousers cfr and and swim back to the 3ft. end brining the trousers with them. The time allowed was five minutes. The winners were: 1, L. Leaity ; 2, W. Ashton; 3, N. Furnival. Ist prize, boudoir timepiece ; 2nd, silver match box ; 3rd, silver knife.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Mayoress afterwards distributed the prizes and thanked the donors on behalf of the committee.</head>
        <p>The Mayoress afterwards distributed the prizes and thanked the donors on behalf of the committee.</p>
        <p>WEDDING AT THE PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Wright—Dale.</p>
        <p>There wss a very pretty wedding at the Parish Church on Thursday afternoon, when Miss Clara Beatrice Dale, second daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Stephen Dale, Elphin Bank. Manchester-road, was married to Mr. Horace J. K. Wright, second son of Dr. Jarvas Wright, Broom. edge, Lymna. The bride and bridegroom are well-known and esteemed, and a large congregation asse nblei to witness the ceremony. The Rev. I. Wadharus, Vicar of Great Houghton, Northampton (uncle of the bridegroom) officiated. Mr. F. M. Darby Mus. Bac., presided at the organ and played choice selections, including Mendelssohn's "Wed- ding March." The bride, who was given away by her father, was charmingly attired in white glace silk trimmed with Brussels lace. She wore a bridal veil and coronet of orange blossom, and carried a lovely white shower bouquet which, together with a gold and amethyst bracelet, was the gift of the bridegroom. The bridesmaids were Miss Edith Wright (sister of the bridegroom), Miss Reeves (cousin of the bridegroom), and Miss Freda Burn (niece of the bride), and they wore beautiful pale blue check voile dresses, and hats of white silk trimmed with white chiffon and pale blue feathers. Misses Wright and Reeves carried bouquets of pink carnations and sweet peas, tied with blue satin ribbon, while Miss Burn carried a baskets of choice flowers. Their ornaments were handsome crescent gold brooches, the gifts of the bridegroom. The bride's mother wore a handsome dress of black brocade trimmed with Brussels lace, with bonnet of mauve, and carried a shower bouquet to match. Mr. J. S. Smith discharged the duties of best man. After the ceremony a reception was held at the Winmarleigh Cafe and the newly- married couple received the hearty congratulations of their friends. Afterwards Mr. and Mrs. Dale enter- tained over 50 guests at breakfast. The happy pair left by motor for Aberystwith (via Chester), where the ' honeymoon is being spent. The bride's going away dress was of brown cloth with cream facings and pastel blue vest and hat en suite. Numerous handsome presents were received.</p>
        <p>Unwholesome beef seized 13,653 Unwholesome mutton seized Unwholesome pork seized 574 Fourteen carcases were surrendered by the owners in consequence of the animals have been affected with tuberculosis. How many were not surrendered, and escaped detection is not known, but sufficient is known to warrant, the statement that the health of Warrington would be safeguarded in this respect by the erection of a public abattoir. Durirg the last few years, considering the backward- ness and wants of the town, very little work of vital importance has been done by the Council. It is true new schools have been built, but this was only owing to the pressurajrom the educational department. Probably the most important feature in connection with the recent history of Warrington bas been the protection of its river rights, and the insistence of the carrying out of the agreement by the Ship Canal Co., to dredge and keep a free passage, but here again it was not the action of the Council that brought this about, but the perseverance and courage of its leading manu- facturers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>CONSTITUTION OF THE COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>THE CONSTITUTION OF THE COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>For really good and effective work for social reform and real advancement of the town, I am of opinion that the Council is divided into too many sections. In our present system of municipal government we probably cannot rid ourselves of the party system, but I think we can fairly ask that the parties should at least send men of character and proved business ability. I know that many would like to see in office men of the stamp of Mr. C. H. Parker, Mr. E. J. Guest, Mr. L. Greening, Mr. W. P. Itylands, Mr. J. Dodds, Mr. H. Roberts, and Mr. F. Nlonks. jun., the two latter of whom ought never to have been allowed to leave the Council. The town is now reaping the benefit of the municipal undertakings for which, assisted by others, they laid such a successful foundation. In conclusion, may I say I have conceived it my duty to acquaint the ratepayers of the town not only with its financial and social condition,as both are relative to each other, hut also in the hope of further arousing public attention to the grave conditions of matters in our midst, and the importance of returning men who can assist in bringing about essential reforms.— Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>E. ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>It's football that works up the muscle And gives a man plenty of dash, It's kick, it's scrum, and it's bustle, And a general looking for lash. In the gobd old hunt for the leather, If a cold you should have to endure, You will soon pull yourself all together. With Woods' Great Peppermint Cure.—Advt.</p>
        <p>BACKHAUS, THE WOR:</p>
