      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>BACKHAUS' VISIf TO WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BACKHAUS' VISIf TO WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>One of the most important of the musical events of the season in Warrington will be the appearance at the Parr Hall on Wednesday evening next of Backhaus, the world renowned pianist. This really great artiste is so well known that he needs no introduction. It is seldom that one so young as Backhaus—he is only 23 —attains such an exalted position in the world of art as that he ilow holds. Backhaus has travelled far, and in distant lands has gained fresh laurels. He has recently returned from a five months tour on the Goa- tinent, during which he gave recitals in over one hundred of the most important towns and cities, being everywhere received with the enthusiasm that is only aroused by the truly, great. During this tour Backhaus has played in eight different countries in fourteen days, viz., Austria, Hungary, Germany, France, Belgium, Holland, Russia</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>NOTES ON GANES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GANES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>4 By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Warringtonians experienced a decided shock on Saturday evening when the news came from St. Helens that the cup-holders had been beaten by 7 points to 6 points. People would hardly credit the news, fcr an easy victory at Knowsley-road had been expected, seeing that Warrington had won every match and St. Helens had lost every one prior t) Saturday. But the Saints once sprung a surprise on Warrington in a cup-tie, and it seems as if Warrington are doomed to a periodical shock at the hands of their neighbours.</p>
        <p>There were reasons on Saturday which account for Warrington's downfall. It was not that St. Helens played well and Warrington badly. No ! the losers were far the superior team at all points, hut they were prevented from running up a respectable score by as peculiar an ,xhibitiou of refereeing on the part of Mr. A. Smith, of Halifax as over I witnessed. He took an objection to the way Le..,1 awl Brooks put the ball in the scrum after the first five minutes, and neither could satisfy him afterwards. Penalty followed penalty until the twc men were so distracted that they refused to play, and then the dear kind gentlem ta in charge of the whistle smiled —and gave anothar penalty. Even the home supporters could see no justice in the referee's decisions, for the Warrington halves appeared to put the ball in with scrupulous care and exactness.</p>
        <p>The result of this extreme attitude—to put it mildly by Mr. Smiths, was that the match degenerated into a inero scramblo wering different coloured jerseys. It was not foothill, though Warrington tried hard to open out the gains, but the sound of the whistle always intervened on the slightest pretext, and often enough without, so far as could be seen from the rails. No team could have given of their best under such circumstances, and if the Northern Union game is to be popularised it will have to be in charge of officials with broader vie,vs than Mr. Smith of Halifax. Warrington have always had a dislike to him since a cedaiu cup-tie with Bradford at Rochdale and the opinion was unanimous among the Warrington players and supporters, that the refereeing on Saturday lost them the game.</p>
        <p>Warrington got a lead early on, after several chances had been missed, and at this stage they looked like winning handsomely They were beating the Saints at every point, and Taylor finally crowned a clever passing bout with a try far oat. Preston made a shocking attempt at goal. From this point to the interval Warrington generally atticked, and had con- sequently to put the ball in the serum nearly every time. No more scoring followed, though Fish might have made a try on his own if he hail been more alert and active. In the second half there was another gleam of football to open with, and Fish got in at the corner alter smart work by the halves and Isherwood. Preston again failed to lift the ball with his place kick. Then the whistling and penalising re-commenced with the result that St. Helens held their own in midfield. ' A piece -of laxity by Taylor proved disastrous, for he put Hillen in possession and a try resulted, which C. Creevey converted with a fine kick. Only a point behind, the hornesters played desperately and Creevey kicked a winning goal from a place quite six yards inside his own half. It was a remarkable shot, even with the breeze, and his failure a few moments later from anotier penalty right in front of the posts at short range was no less remarkable. Warrington endeavoured to secure the lead again and twice reached the line, oaly be as surely penalised and see St. Helens escape. Fish just failed with a shot from near touch, and Warrington had to admit a bitter defeat by the narrowest margin.</p>
        <p>Little need be said about the players. Brooks was the best, and Fish and Preston the worst of the War- rington lot. Dickenson, to), was far from being a success on the wing. So long as the whistle was silent the Warrington men played perfect football, but it was only for a few minutes that they had the chance. St. Helens played with vigour but with no skill, Toole, C. Creevey and Briers being the best of a rather ragged lot.</p>
        <p>The defeat, of course, lost Warrington two valuable pointi in the g3rthern, South Lancashire, and Lanca- shire Leagues. In all prooability it will span their chance of success in either of the two latter competi- t;ona, especially seeing that Wigan won at ltuncirn on Saturday.</p>
        <p>The following is the Northern League tible up•to-date _ For. Agst. Per-</p>
        <p>P. NV. L. D. PLS. G. T. P. Q. T. P. c't'ge. Hunslet 4 4 0 0 8 9 9 45 2 3 13 100.00 Oldham 5 5 0 0 10 16 19 89 3 6 24 10i)'00 Wigan 5 4 1 0 8 913 57 8 7 37 80.00 Warrington 4 3 1 0 6 814 58 3 3 15 75.00 York, Broughton 4 3 1 0 6 911 51 6 5 27 7500 Hull 6 4 2 0 818 17 80 8 8 40 66.66 Brarnley Rtrrow 3 2 1 0 4 310 36 3 7 27 66.66 Halifax Batley 5 3 2 0 6 3 5 21 6 4 21 6000 Salford</p>
        <p>Leeds llewsbuiy 4 2 2 0 1 7 0 32 11 10 52 5000 Hull K. R .l 2 2 0 412 10 54 5 6 28 50'00 Keighley 4 2 2 0 4 6 8 36 6 5 27 50'0 Wakefield 4 2 2 0 410 10 68 911 51 5000 Leigh Swinton Bradford Huddersfield 4 1 3 0 2 4 4 20 810 46 25.00 St. Helens 4 1 3 0 2 3 4 18 617 63 25110 Widnes 4 1 3 0 2 6 12 48 8 14 53 . 2500 Rochdale 4 0 4 0 0 3 6 24 13 10 56 0000 Ebbw Vale 4 0 4 0 0 2 5 19 14 .22 94 0000 Merthyr Runcorn</p>
        <p>T e County trial match at Wcrrington on Monday provided a fine struggle between Probables and Pogsibles which the latter won by 17-11. Brooks, Isherwood and Fish were on the winning side, and had a lot to do with the victory. Brooks was the best half on the field, and he and his partner secured</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>D-RENOWNED PIANIST.</head>
        <p>D-RENOWNED PIANIST.</p>
        <p>and England. Surely this must constitute a record. A special honour accorded to Backhaus was his selec- tion by H.S.H. Prince Karl of Schwarzburg-Sonder- hausen to give a demonstrilion and lecture course at the pianoforte, "Four weeks with the great Masters," at the Sonderhausen Conservatoire. So successful did this course prove that Backhaus was presented with a gold medal for merit. The honour bestowed upon Backhaus by the Prince is a very important one, he having been selected from a number of the leading pianists of the day. Backhaus will be assisted in his recital in Warring- ton next Wednesday by Miss Ellen Forena, a soprano who has made a great success recently in Paris, and Herr Bobber, one of the most distinguished of the younger generation of violinists. The concert promises to be one of a most interesting and delightful nature and early application br tickets should be made to Mr. R. H. Pearson, 57, Bridge-street.</p>
        <p>selection in preference to White (Oldham) and Jolley. Fish scored two fine tries. but he is still far from being in proper condition, and will have to get his weight down before he can give of his best.</p>
        <p>The County teatn to melt Cumberland at Broughton to-day Wa afterwards chosen as follows : —Gifford (Barrow); Fish (Warrington), Wilson (Broughton Rangers), Jenkins and Leytharn (Wigan); Thomas (Wigan); Brooks (Warrington) • Spencer (Salford), Beetham (Broughton Rangers), O'Neil (Leigh), Hannah (Barrow), Padbury (Ru.ncorn), and Simister (Swinton).</p>
        <p>Both Warrington and Oldham will suffer from the claims of the County match, for W. Dixon, Ferguson, and Owens have been chosen from the Oldham side to represent Cumberland, while Warrington have Fish and Brooks on the Lancashire team. In addition Taylor will be an absentee, as he was injured at St. Helens, so that it is fortunate the club is strong in reserve talent. There is quite a big change in the compsitiou of the team, for Heath, Harmer and Preston have been dropped. The selected side is : Tilley, full back; Jordan, Isherwood, Cartwright and Dickenson threequarters ; Lees and Jenkins half backs; A. Boardman, P. Boardman, Thomas, Shugars Naylor and Belton forwards.</p>
        <p>The Oldham team will be: R L. Thomas ; Tyson, B. Dixon, Llewellyn ; Oldershaw ; Bevnen and White; Avery (captain), Smith, Topham, Wright, Longvvorth, and Wilkinson. Reserve : Groves.</p>
        <p>The" A " team will have a severe task, and could probably do very well with some of the points they scored in such profusion last Saturday. By the way the score of 12 goals, 18 tries (78 points) against St. Helens" A " was a record for the club and ground. Forster performed a fine goal-kicking feat in landing 9 voals in succession and a couple more later on. The team to visit Oldham to-day will contain a young wing three-quarter named Harrison from Runcorn Fertilizera, who was- Biped on last week-end, and from whom great things are expected. He is a very speedy runner and was sought after by Runcorn and Wigan. The selected team is : Fie mitt, full blck ; Harrison, Kenyon. Landor, and Harris, three-quarters; Kaye and Daintith, half backs ; Heath, Preston, Harmer, Jolley, Fairhurst, and Holbrook, forwards.</p>
        <p>Oldhain " A. " to trilet Warrington' A." will be : —Tetlow ; Taylor, Irvine, Wiltshire, Barker; A. Lees, Yewlett ; Doyle, Bowers, Civil, Malley, M'Llan, and Lloyd. Reserve : Mitten.</p>
        <p>Brooks will be unahle to play for the County to-day alter all. The sprained ankle he sustained in the trial game has proved troublesome, and he was reluctantly. compelled to" cry off" yesterday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL'</head>
        <p>RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL'</p>
        <p>Brook Pollution at Padgate.</p>
        <p>The 'monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural District Council was held at Bewsey Chambers on Wednesday afternoon, Dr. Nlitrrav presiding. There were also present :—Nlessrs. %V. Houghton, J. Back- house, A. Brooks, W. Purness, G. Mather, C. Sutton (clerk), S. Suttoit (assistant clerk), B. S. Newcombe (surveyor), H. H. Burnage (assistant surveyor), Dr. Sephton (medical officer), and F. Kerfoot (inspector of nuisances).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>LOW DEATH RATE.</head>
        <p>LOW DEATH RATE.</p>
        <p>Dr. SEPEITON reported that during the mouth ending October 2nd there had been 19 births and 0 deaths in the district. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 8•8 per 1,003 against 333 the average of the last three months and against 23 the correspinding month of last year. Seven deaths occurred in the County Asylum, Winwick, so that the death-rate proper of the district was equal to 19 per 1,030. The following cases of infectious diseases were notified :—Scarlet fever, 14 ; diptheria, 1 ; enteric fever, 1 ; 3rysipeks, 3. Several members, commented upon the very satis- factory character of the report, the death rate being remarkably low.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>BROOK POLLUTION.</head>
        <p>BROOK POLLUTION.</p>
        <p>The CLERIC read the following letter from Hr. Hugh Stowell., chef inspector to the Mersey and Irwell Joint Committee, Manchester :—ln January, 1936, my pre- decessor, Mr. Tattoo, had correspondence with you with reference to the pollution of the Padgate and Orford brooks by the discharge of untreated sewage from Fearnhead and Padgate. You promised that the subject should receive the consideration of your Council. I should be glad to hear from you whether after such consideration your Council have any scheme in view for stopping this pollution. In the couse of his letter in Jannary 1901 i, Mr.Tatton stated: It seems desirable thst sewage works should be put down to deal with these districts,or, what would probably be more convenient, that arrangements should be made for admitting the sewage into the Warring- ton sewers. The pollution in this district has increased considerably during recent years. During the discussion it was stated that when the question was brought up some time ago the Corpora- tion were approached with a view to taking the sewage into their sewers, but the Corporation found that their sewers were incapable of taking any more. Dr. SEPFITON said the brooks were in a very bad state and required immediate attention. He sirggested that if the Corporation would not take the sewage, two bacteria beds should be constructed, one for the Orford sewage and the other for Padgate and Fearnhead. A committee consisting of Mr. G. Mather, Mr. P. Longton, the Medical Officer, the Surveyor and the Inspector, was appointed to investigate the matter and report.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>ANOTHER SEWAGE QUESTION.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER SEWAGE QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The Surtvi-I:o—R—PiOdUced plan; deposited by Mr. W. Walker, architect, of 20 new houses which it waa pro- posed to erect in Padgate-lane, Poulton-with-Fearnbead. So far as the buildings were concerned, the plans were in accordance with the byelaws, hut a question had arisen as to the disposal of sewage. After a lengthy consideration, the Council deferred the passing of the plans and a small committee was appointed to endea‘,our to make satisfactory arrange- ments with Mr. Walker for the sewering. Subject to that, dig plans will be passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>"TOO OLD" AT 35.</head>
        <p>"TOO OLD" AT 35.</p>
        <p>That is what the man feels who is at all troubled with kidney complaints. An aching back destroys both mental and bodily energy, and takes all pleasure away from life. In any such case the following simple home-made remedy ought to be worth its weight in gold. Ask your chemist to mix you one ounce of Tincture of Buchu, one ounce of Karox Compound, and two ounces of Syrup of Lemon; or simply buy them and mix them yourself at home, shaking them together in a bottle. Try a teaspoonful after each meal and at bedtime. The expense is little, and the effect is sur- prising if the prescription is taken regularly for a week or two.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>"THE ALL BLACKS."</head>
        <p>"THE ALL BLACKS."</p>
        <p>ARRIVAL OF THE TEAM IN LEEDS, The First Practice. INTERVIEW WITH MR. H, ASHTON.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>The professional "All Blacks" have come. That "phantom team " Mr. Wray Palliser has been writing so glibly and ignorantly about, arrived at Folkestone on Monday afteroon at 1.35. There they were met by a deputation of Northern Union officials headed by Mr. H. Ashton of Warrington, the President of the Union. So far from being a phantom team, the impression created was that New Zealand had sent over a remarkably fine-looking body of 28 athletes in the pride of their physical strength. Their captain, H. Wright, a fine forward scaling over 13 stones, was soon interviewed and had no hesitation in expressing an opinion on his team. Said he genially: "Some people say that the Northern Union cannot be beaten. We have come over to England to say that we intend to do it! At any rate, we shall have a mighty big try. When the All Blacks were playing in England the Northern Union claimed that they bad a team that could beat the' New Zealanders. We shall now give them the opportunity of proving of what they are capable against New Zealand footballers."_ _ _ .</p>
        <p>After staying all night in London, the party left for Leeds, the headquarters of the team, on Tuesday afternoon, arriving at the Yorkshire city at 8 p.m. Here they had a magnificent reception, the whole population having apparently crowded round the station to give the wearers of the fern a typical York- shire welcome. They were amazed at the interest of the people in them and G. W. Smith, the crack three- quarter who was over here with the previous team remarked to Mr. Ashton that it was a better reception than over the original" All Blacks" got during their memorable tour. Tuesday evening was devoted to a banquet given by the Northern Union to their guests, and some interesting speeches were made. Gleeson, one of the New Zealand half-backs, and a college graduate, proved quite an orator. On Wednesday morning the serious business of the tour commenced when the whole band of 28 players turned out at 10.30 for their first practice on the Headingley ground. It was to learn about this opening exhibition and the evidences of form that we interviewed Mr. H. Ashton on Thursday. We found the popular Warring- ton secretary in a chatty mood, and very favourably impressed with the" All Blacks." They are," said Mr. Ashton, "a very intelligent body of men, and a fine band of athletes. Every man is full of enthusiasm and keenness for the game. As a team they are young, the majority being mei' who are approaching their best." As an earnest of their good faith, he added, each man has put down £5O towards the expenses of the trip. They will receive 70 per cent. of the gross takings in every match. How did the men look in the field? we asked.</p>
        <p>"Fine fellows, without a superfluous ounce of flesh, and in grand trim considering the time they have been on board ship," replied Mr. Ashton. "They strip remarkably well. Five forwards at least take the field at 13 stones 4lbs. or just over. "On Tuesday morning they had two hours runnieg about, punting, catching, and passing the ball, without a sign of leg-weariness. Every man went at it at full speed, and their all-round pace was a noticeable eature. The passing was first-class, ten or a dozen men taking the ball from end to end at full speed without ever dropping it or getting an inch out of place. They all seemed very sure catchers, and good kickers."</p>
        <p>" What about Messenger ? " the Australian crack who has joined the team, we asked. " Messenger is a man about the build of Lomas," said Mr. Ashton. "He is a very muscular fellow, and. „ can play either three-quarter or five-eigntas.ne struck me as being very fast and very clever. "The two Wynyards both seem very smart, and the younger particularly good at dodging," he a dded. "Wrigley is another three-quarter who shoal d make an impression. He is about sft. llin. high, 13 stones 4 lb in weight, and tremendously powerful and fast for such a position. Smith also showed great clever- ness."</p>
        <p>"Are they going to adopt the new Zealand formation?"</p>
        <p>" Twit is not settled yet. But the men are very methodical and close students of the game. Under Mr. J. H. Smith of Widnes they will aeon pick up the points of our play and then decide which style tl adopt. They have not brought a wing forward with them, recognizing that wing forward play is not encouraged in the Northern Union. Rugby football is their national game, very little Association football being played in New Zealand. Four of the team played in the side which defeated the All Blacks before they set sail for England two years ago, and four of them were members of the touring team." "How do you think they will fare against the Northern Union teams ? " we inquired at the close. "Our teams will find them a very hard nut to crack. They will beat more than beat them "said Mr. Ashton with an air of conviction. "All they ask for is a fair field and no favour." We left Mr. Ashton with a word of thanks for his. courtesy. Our readers can rest assured that they will learn something from the visitors, and find them worthy foemen. A: better estimate of their prowes3 will be available when they have met Brainley 013- Wednesday next, and Huddersfield on the Saturday. I lope to see the Branaley match, and form my own oninione as to the professional Rugby pioneers of New Zealand.</p>
        <p>• The following are the names of the players who have been brought over and the positions in which they usually play. It must be understood, however, that the backs, three-quarters, and five-eighths are all available to play in any of the back positions : BACKS.</p>
        <p>S. Turtill (International). H. Rowe (Auckland County). XHREE-QUARTERS.</p>
        <p>G. Smith (International). E. Wrigley (International). D. 111`Gregor (International). J. Lavery (North Island). L. Todd (Auckland County). FIVE-EIGHTHS. H. Messenger (International). R. Wvnyard (Auckland County). W. Wynyard (Auckland County). HALVES. A. Kelly (Wellington County). .1. Gleeson (Napier). W. Tyler (Auckland County). E. Tyme (South Island). FORWARDS. W. Johnson (International). T. Cross (International). W. Mackrell (international). E. Watkins (International). C. Pearce (South Island representative). C. Byrne (Wellington County). D. Gilchrist (Wellington County). W. Trevarthen (Auckland County). C. Dunning (North Island). H. Wright (Wellington County). A. bile (North Island). A. Callam (Wellington County). D. • Fraser (Petone). _ _</p>
        <p>H. Baskerville (Wellington County). In the foregoing list it is to be noted that all the' players, who are defined as North Island or South Island representatives, are really internationals in the sense that games between England and Scotland ar°. spoken of as international.</p>
      </div>
