me correct and in order, smoke prevention adopted by the Associated Chambers Having, however, been elected,and taken upon myself of Commerce. the duties of elective auditor, I consider that the rate- OFFICERS RE-ELECTED. payers and citizens expect more than a mere statement Mr. C. MADELEY moved the re-election of the officers i that the accounts are correct, and therefore beg to sub- of the Society as follows :—President, Colonel Robert mit to you, through the courtesy of the Press, the Ireland Blackburne, CB.; Executive Committee, Rev. 'following further report and review of the town. Canon Stevenson, chairman; Alderman Arthur Bennett, Captain Crosfleld, Rev. C. Harvey Cook, Mr. John Fair- THE COMMERCIAL ASPECT OF THE hurst, Mr. Linnieus Greening, Mr. E. James Hall, Chun- CORPORATION. cillor Dr. Joseph, Dr. Markel, Mr. J. Moore Murmy,Mr. J. Ryan, Mr. W. Silcock, Councillor J. Fulton-Smith, Reviewing the status or position of the town, I consider the truest and fairest way is to see how it Mr. John Upson.; hon. treasurer, Mr. J. Longland, Egypt-street; secretary, Mr. G. H. Smith, 3, Winwick- compares with the other cities and towns of England and Wales, and to draw conclusions therefrom. I have • street. Councillor WsLsoN seconded and said he had brought before me a list of 89 of the chief towns, including Warrington. I find, although the ratable value of War- which sent forth great volumes of smoke. He hoped rington is on the low side, that 59 of the towns are something would be done to mitigate the nuisance. higher rated, Warrington standing 30th on the list. I The resolution was carried. also find with reference to the undertakings owned Votes of thanks were accorded to the officers of the by the Corporation, that only one town in England and Society and to the chairman for presiding. Wales contributes more proportionately from its under- takings towards the reduction of its rates than War- rington does, Warrington thus taking second place in the list of 89 towns, Tnis really is a gseat testimony not only to the value of the undertakings, but also to the efficiency_ of the various departments of the Cor- poration. Very few towns of the size of ,Warrington can show better results from their gas tramways. and electric undertakings; in fact; many of these in some towns are working at a loss. I may add, too, that the charges made in Warrington for gas, water, and electric ii ht will compare very favourably with most. I think the gas department desires special mention, for in face of a reduction in the price of gas and the increased price in coal (the management having had the foresight to purchase well in advance), over £14,000 was made, after making provision for the usual sinking fund. £ll,OOO of this sum will go to the town in relief of the rates. It is therefore evident that, so far as the manage- ment of our undertakings is concerned, we have nothing to learn commercially from other towns, and are, in fact, an example in this to almost all. THE SANITARY OR HEALTH SIDE op THE TOWN. It is when we consider the health, social and sanitary condition of the town that we find very grave and sad defects in the management of the town. In fact, the .town in this respect is in a deplorable state, and is a disgrace and a scandal not only, to the Corporation, but also to the citizens. Let us compare its vital statistics with the other towns of England and Wales : Annual Rates per 1,000 Living : Deaths Infant Deaths from Mortality Births from seven under 1 all causes. chief year per Epidemic 1000. Diseases. England and Wales 271 ... 154 ... 171 ... 133 76 Great Towns 271 ... 151 214 ... 145 142 Smaller Towns 26'9 ... 145 I'7l ... 138 England and Wales less the 218 towns 26'3 ... 14'1 ... I'lB ... 110 Warrington I consider that a perusal and careful thought over the above reliable statistics should make every rate- payer and citizen in Warrington reflect, and not only reflect, but do what he can to bring about a better con- dition of things. Whatever may be said to the contrary, there is no getting over the fact that Warrington is one of the unhealthiest towns in England. It is not that it stands out in this respect only for one year, for the returns years after year are practically the same in this respect. The chief cause for this state of things is the insanitary conditions in which the people have to live. In Warrington there is too much slum property, and if the Corporation means to do its duty to the inhabitants under its management, much further demolition of this class of property will have to take place. In passing, may I say that it is to be regretted that there are to be found certain selfish property owners, as evidenced from the recent inquiry, backed up by well-known councillors who place all the obstacles in the way of the Corporation making improvements in this respect in the town, and are thus not only hinder- ing its progress, but are also, in a great measure, rsspon- Bible for its continued deplorable health condition. I have myself made several inspections of the streets, passages, and courts in Warrington, and I regret to find that the condition is almost indescribable. Sunlight never enters some of the windows of some homes owing to the close surroundings of other houses. Not only have passages and courts never been paved, but some of the houses have not even the ordinary conveniences of civilization. Children of tender years play amongst filth and garbage thrown from the doors • therefore, no wonder that epidemics and fevei.s of all kinds are so prevalent. Measles, like the poor, are always in our midst. One might easily write volumes on the subject, but I would advise the ratepayers to peruse the reports of the late Dr. Gornall, and also the one issued this year by Dr. Prudence E. Gaffficin, on the state of Warrington's public health for 1906. One is forcibly borne to the conclusion that many of our councillors (Alderman Bennett, Councillor Stott and a few others being notable exceptions) appear to think it more meritorious to aim at low rates than at a low death-rate, for they are always ready to talk about the former, but never about the latter. They live in a fool's paradise, for the ratepayers in the long run have ,to pay very dearly for the town's insanitary and I unhealthy condition. 1 Referring to the health question, may I draw atten- tion to the great need of a public abattoir in the town? In spite of the vigilance of the officials large quantities of unwholesome meat have been surreptitiously con- veyed into the town, and the poor people especially are victimized from these sources, though the richer class do not escape scot free. I know very well that some butchers will object to these remarks, but no respectable and honest butcher will, for I know of cases where butchers have refused to take a doubtful carcase, but where others have not been so scrupulous or squeamish. However, let the official report speak for itself :</p>
